00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.490
for the last four weeks who know has the pleasure of
talking to you about what I do.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.940
So I'm an assistant professor here at the Child Study Center and I'm the associate director

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:33.190
of the Developmental electrophysiology laboratory,
which is run by Linda Mays and we kinda of

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.530
pool our resources cause we all apply the same

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.499
methods to study

00:00:37.499 --> 00:00:39.449
child development,

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:41.580
both typical and atypical development.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.740
And we also all are kind of interested

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.030
in social development

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.390
for different kinds of reasons. And I should also acknowledge Michael Crowley, who is the other associate director

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.460
of the DEL.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.470
So when we start a talk like this in the medical school, 

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.750
we make disclosures, we

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.719
present everything that gives us a bias.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:02.560
So there's the financial biases. These are the people who fund

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.540
the research that we do. So I get

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.080
money to do my research from

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.290
a couple of different government institutes and I will soon from NARSAD.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.560
And I also get money from book sales.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.830
And then in this particular instance I want
to disclose

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.670
a non-financial

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.250
conflict of interest

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.480
because

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.540
we,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.720
all the presenters, you've heard us kind of gripe about this, it's weird for us. What we do as clinical psychologists

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.650
is show lots of pictures of people

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.409
and we show you videos of people and they make things interesting.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:34.660
They keep the people who are listening to you awake, which is something that we all like.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.369
We can't do that for this,

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:39.920
but for me it's a different situation because
every moment

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.279
that I am speaking to an audience

00:01:42.279 --> 00:01:45.759
and showing them pictures of people who are
not my children,

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:47.700
I am experiencing internal tension.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.670
So now I have the nice circumstance, where I have only the choice

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.229
to show you pictures

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:55.400
of people who's right to consent

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.870
I control.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.780
And so you'll be seeing lots of

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.009
Nora

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:00.670
and Agnus

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.400
in the next hour or so.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.380
So what I'm going to talk about today

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:06.390
is

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.110
the research that I

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.539
have been doing here. I have been here since
2004. I first came here as a,

00:02:12.539 --> 00:02:16.629
as a clinical intern. I'm a clinical psychologist
and then I did a post-doc.

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:19.519
And then I've been on the research faculty and the regular
faculty,

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:20.889
since then.

00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:21.559
So I,

00:02:21.559 --> 00:02:22.260
I am

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.450
very interesting autism and the way people
with autism process information because

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.950
I think of autism

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.709
as a social disorder. I am very interested in the way they process social information.

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:36.059
And one of the best ways to study social information
processing is with faces.

00:02:36.059 --> 00:02:38.709
So we're going to start off talking some about 

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:41.799
behavioral and brain specialization for faces.

00:02:41.799 --> 00:02:45.040
And then I'm going to talk a little bit about electrophysiology,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.629
about the method of electrophysiology because

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:49.090
it's not familiar to

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.059
everybody and some of the things about it are a little

00:02:51.059 --> 00:02:52.629
idiosyncratic.

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:54.670
I'm going to talk about face perception

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:59.090
as a tool to understand autism and social development in autism

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.259
and whether

00:03:00.259 --> 00:03:04.170
the problems that we see in autism are better
explained as a,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:09.470
a general information processing problem or some kind of specific problem,  a specific problem to social

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.069
information.

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:14.599
And then I'm going to talk about some of the work
that's going on now and some of the things

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:17.029
that we have in store. And

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:17.519
please

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:23.719
do interrupt just because the cameras are rolling doesn't
mean that you can't ask questions, so please do.

00:03:23.719 --> 00:03:28.069
I, maybe I'll try to be unclear so you have to ask questions.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:31.649
So when we think about autism, and this should all be familiar to you now,

00:03:31.649 --> 00:03:33.909
and when we think about autism

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:35.219
as problem

00:03:35.219 --> 00:03:36.370
in three areas:

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.930
so social

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.659
problems,

00:03:38.659 --> 00:03:40.549
problems with communication

00:03:40.549 --> 00:03:44.399
and that can be lots of different kinds of problems with
communication. It could mean that you just

00:03:44.399 --> 00:03:45.759
don't talk.

00:03:45.759 --> 00:03:47.540
It could mean

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:48.679
that you

00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:51.620
have lots of language, but have problems communicating
with people,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.589
but mostly resolving, revolving around social

00:03:54.589 --> 00:03:55.559
communication.

00:03:55.559 --> 00:03:57.649
And then a third category

00:03:57.649 --> 00:04:00.959
that's a, a, a lot more nebulous

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:02.689
and less well defined.

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:07.399
We describe this as rigid, repetitive behaviors
and interests. And this can be anything ranging

00:04:07.399 --> 00:04:08.889
from

00:04:08.889 --> 00:04:11.670
repetitive motor movements like hand flapping or

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.090
being really insistent

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:16.680
on a particular schedule or particular routine,

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.220
so those kinds of things, so.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.639
And when we have problems in all those areas, we describe them as autism spectrum disorders.

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:25.599
One of things that's really

00:04:25.599 --> 00:04:26.000
crucial

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.569
to think about when you think about autism

00:04:28.569 --> 00:04:31.469
is that it happens in a developing child.

00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:34.779
So autism isn't just something that happens,

00:04:34.779 --> 00:04:35.240
right.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.849
It's something that a person is most likely
born with,

00:04:37.849 --> 00:04:40.139
but then they carry that through them,

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:42.160
through, carry that with them

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.539
as they, they move through the world.

00:04:44.539 --> 00:04:46.509
And in that respect

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:50.530
autism shapes the world the person lives in. So when whatever is different

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:52.240
about their brain when they're born,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.849
it's also affected by a very different world

00:04:54.849 --> 00:04:56.419
that they live in.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:57.310
So there's the kind of

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:58.780
primary

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.320
things that first shape the brain,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.039
but then there's a secondary things

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:05.280
that a person experiences as they move through
the world,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.939
as a person with autism.

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.990
So if we think about

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.919
typical social development

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:14.360
kind of representing this course. So

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.330
we come into the world

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:17.460
really attuned

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.289
to people.

00:05:18.289 --> 00:05:19.949
And I'm going to talk about,

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:22.729
I know you are, you all are going to have a face,

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:26.260
face development overload because Kasha talked about some of it

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.889
already, but I'll try to talk about some of
the facts

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:33.309
that she, she didn't touch upon because these are some of the things I think are really coolest

00:05:33.309 --> 00:05:34.610
about human development.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.120
But I'll talk about,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.470
we cut, how we come into the world really paying
close attention to people

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:40.639
and we remain that way.

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:43.259
For most of us people are really what make
the world

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:45.139
go around

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:49.180
and we spend our lives thinking and learning
about people. We become social experts,

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.300
but we can think about people,

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.440
well, people with autism, for example, 

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.939
charting a different course,

00:05:54.939 --> 00:05:56.840
so kind of becoming expert

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:58.119
in other things, so

00:05:58.119 --> 00:06:00.519
paying attention to

00:06:00.519 --> 00:06:03.389
things besides people, learning about those
things,

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:06.500
becoming experts other things.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.040
What's important to think about,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.490
about this kind of way of understanding autism, 

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.290
if we're not saying

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.229
the this is a brain

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:17.069
that can't do certain kinds of things,

00:06:17.069 --> 00:06:20.370
we're saying it's a brain that is paying attention
to the wrong kinds

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:21.479
of information,

00:06:21.479 --> 00:06:25.299
which is a crucial distinction when we think about
the different kinds of theories

00:06:25.299 --> 00:06:29.369
that people have put forward to explain autism

00:06:29.369 --> 00:06:31.639
they can kind of grossly fall into two categories:

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:35.190
the idea there's something wrong with social
information processing

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.520
or the idea that this is a brain that can't handle

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.870
the nature of

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.699
processing

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:42.510
of things that happen to be social.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.550
And by that I mean, for example,

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:45.889
complex information,

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:50.560
the idea that people with autism only have trouble
with social information because it's complex

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.780
versus there being something uniquely social about it.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.080
And so when we look at it this way

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.799
we're saying that this is a brain that can do many sophisticated,
many complex things,

00:06:58.799 --> 00:07:00.769
it just can't do them

00:07:00.769 --> 00:07:02.839
with social information.

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:04.470
And so,

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.579
as a, a person is interested in development, when
we look

00:07:07.579 --> 00:07:08.120
at these,

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:09.969
these pathways, right,

00:07:09.969 --> 00:07:13.069
we think of this person getting older at
they move along

00:07:13.069 --> 00:07:18.129
and we want to understand how we can trace it further back, right. If we think about this person

00:07:18.129 --> 00:07:20.439
the further they get down here

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:23.860
the harder time they're having with many of the things
that really matter

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:27.069
in the world cause a lot of the things right now that matter in the world from

00:07:27.069 --> 00:07:28.579
having friends to,

00:07:28.579 --> 00:07:33.069
to checking out of a super market involve people and so one of the ways we study this is with faces

00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:36.729
because we can learn so much about faces very early in life

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:40.889
and we can apply it throughout the lifespan. So it's a good way for us

00:07:40.889 --> 00:07:42.830
as people interested in child development

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.559
to try to get down to the brass tacks in terms
of where things

00:07:46.559 --> 00:07:48.660
start both in healthy social development

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.409
and in terms of where things go awry

00:07:51.409 --> 00:07:52.919
in atypical social development.

00:07:52.919 --> 00:07:54.689
So let's talk some about

00:07:54.689 --> 00:07:55.510
faces

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.310
in typical social development.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.530
So we know that faces are probably thee
first way,

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.330
I guess sound too,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.230
and some of my colleagues have interesting theories about the way 

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:07.270
sound could shape

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:10.629
social development even within the womb, but

00:08:10.629 --> 00:08:12.469
conventionally faces are one of the first ways

00:08:12.469 --> 00:08:15.189
that we learn to interact with other people, that we

00:08:15.189 --> 00:08:16.349
about other people.

00:08:16.349 --> 00:08:20.949
and we also know a lot about faces, so

00:08:20.949 --> 00:08:26.110
developmental psychologists, cognitive neuro-scientists,

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:29.419
neurologists have all done a lot of great
research to help us understand

00:08:29.419 --> 00:08:32.490
the way people approach faces, both in early infancy

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.739
and throughout the lifespan. So it's a really nice

00:08:34.739 --> 00:08:36.379
comparison for autism, 

00:08:36.379 --> 00:08:39.440
which is we think it has something to do with social,

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:40.530
social interaction

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.859
that we think affects us very early
in life

00:08:42.859 --> 00:08:45.449
and that we think carries us, carries through lifespan, 

00:08:45.449 --> 00:08:48.210
but we know very little about in terms of the neuro bases.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.050
And so faces are a nice way to kind of

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:52.250
tap in

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:56.290
to those areas that are, that have yet to be explained
in autism.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:59.780
So, just some of the, I guess I wouldn't even call them facts, but you can call them factoids

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:02.320
about faces, bless you,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.440
is that

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.020
very early in life, so infants nine minutes old

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:12.620
are more likely to look at faces or things that are, even things that are just like faces. So 

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:14.320
if you take

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.170
a schematic face, just with two squares on the top and

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:23.670
and one square on the bottom, kind of like two eyes and a mouth, but not even the right shape, just the configuration,

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.070
infants are going to look at that

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.800
more than other things.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.030
They are going to track it further.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:33.380
So if you move one across an infant's field of vision, they're going to follow it further.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:34.940
And this is compared to

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:40.620
even an upside down one or a bull's eye, you can take lots of things that you might be interesting, 

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.650
that you might think would be interesting to infants,
all the Fisher Price toys.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.470
Oh we're going to have to take that out of the youtube cut,

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.379
all the Fisher Price toys and

00:09:49.379 --> 00:09:52.550
a Toys"R"Us are not as interesting to most infants

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.430
as, as faces.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:59.910
We also know that babies are more likely to smile at human faces.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:01.680
This is my older daughter

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.160
doing her best pirate impression as a five-week-old.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.580
We know that,

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:10.660
that again infants seem to enjoy looking
at faces, which is a great tool.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:15.560
So how many people in this room have ever interacted
with a really small baby, say a five-week-old baby?

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.600
So if you do something

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:18.540
to that baby 

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:23.170
and that baby smiles at you, what do you do? 

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.740
I'm sorry.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.310
You smile back. You do it again.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:27.459
I've spent

00:10:27.459 --> 00:10:30.190
over the past three years tremendous amounts
of time

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.619
making stupid faces and stupid sounds

00:10:32.619 --> 00:10:34.320
to make this happen again.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.530
And so here is this little helpless baby

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.350
that has this one tool they can use

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:40.310
that can then control

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:45.480
the behavior of an adult very effectively, right.
So what a great way

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:50.940
to, to draw the kind of information that we need
to develop. If people talking to us and people

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.579
smiling at us and making silly sounds

00:10:52.579 --> 00:10:54.500
it's going to, what's going to help us grow.

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.440
It's pretty amazing that human infants come into the world 

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:00.840
able to elicit that information from their environment.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.490
So by the second day

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:06.050
very young children are able to recognize the
face of their mother

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:08.840
and these have been very elegant studies  

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:14.000
controlling for whether a person has also
just given birth, so it's not new motherness they're

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:14.649
recognizing.

00:11:14.649 --> 00:11:17.290
It's not the scent of their mother they're recognizing.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:21.350
It's not her blondness or her burnettness or her
blue-eyed-ness.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:22.699
They're recognizing 

00:11:22.699 --> 00:11:23.729
the face of their mother

00:11:23.729 --> 00:11:26.180
by the second day of life.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:27.340
And in one of my,

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:31.790
my favorite studies of all time, this is a study done by Andy Meltzoff

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:34.220
who is at the University of Washington, who

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:38.430
I was fortunate to study with as a
graduate student.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.080
And what Andy did was

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:44.800
he partnered with a maternity ward in a hospital in Seattle

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.840
and he found some parents

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:50.760
who were apparently very, very motivated to help
science

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.870
and they got his beeper number

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.550
and when the baby was born

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:56.630
and he was beeped

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.380
and whether it was noon

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.750
or whether it was midnight or whether it was three a.m. or nine a.m.,

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:05.260
Andy would zip down to the hospital and
that newborn baby

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.280
would be put in a, a baby carrier

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.170
and Andy would hover over the baby

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.830
with a video camera behind him

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:13.780
filming the baby

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:18.520
but not filming Andy and then Andy would do things to the baby. He would stick out his tongue.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:21.400
He would pucker his lips. He would open his mouth, 

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.610
all while the video camera is recording the baby.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:27.640
So then he would take these videotapes back
to his research assistants

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.690
and they would code what the baby's face was doing

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.989
and sure enough you were able to predict 

00:12:32.989 --> 00:12:35.010
what face Andy had been making

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.430
by looking at the baby.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:42.310
So, the youngest kids that they saw this happening
in were kids who were forty-two minutes old.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:46.160
So what's so remarkable about this is that

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:50.380
when we think about kind of the cliche of nature
versus nurture, there hasn't

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:52.150
been a lot of nurturing 

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:54.610
that's happened in forty-two minutes, right.

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:56.710
So these are kids who

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:59.760
haven't had a ton of time to learn about their own face.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:03.029
They haven't had a ton of time to learn about other people's
faces

00:13:03.029 --> 00:13:06.690
and yet they're able to see what's happening
there,

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:07.930
understand what's,

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:09.680
what that is, what's there

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:11.180
is what I've got here

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:12.470
and what that is doing

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:13.800
I can do.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:14.990
And Andy really

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:16.230
takes this as

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:20.300
the, the basis for all social interaction, the idea he calls

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:22.250
the "Like Me" hypothesis, the idea that

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:23.720
children are born

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:24.660
with a,

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:29.450
with an internal and intramodal, and, and intramodal map, so that they can kind of map on

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:30.470
what they see

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:31.980
to their sense of their body.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:38.980
And he thinks that that's from where social interaction
emerges.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.430
So then we also know that,

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:43.380
we theorize 

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:45.309
the face processing also

00:13:45.309 --> 00:13:49.250
gives us a foundation for much more sophisticated

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:51.090
social interaction skills.

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:53.670
So does everyone know what theory of mind is?

00:13:53.670 --> 00:14:00.670
Who can tell me what theory of mind is? That is the idea that others have their own ideas and beliefs. Exactly.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:15.810
Right, that that other people have their own intentions, their own wants, their own needs, their own beliefs.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:20.900
And so, one of the ideas for the development of a
theory of mind and, and Simon Baron Cohen, who's a very

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.160
productive autism researcher

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.490
has written eloquently about this,

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:30.540
is that under, understanding other people's faces and understanding other people's eyes 

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:33.870
is the first way we can learn about other
people's intentions.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:36.510
And by understanding other people's intentions, we can then

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:40.040
build the idea that their intentions aren't necessarily the same as ours

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.440
and that's the basis of a theory of mind.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.610
So we've talked about these kind of 

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.160
early,

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.870
these aspects of early development that are really precocious.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:54.540
We're very good with faces before we're very good with many other things

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:55.790
and as we develop

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.940
we see that the human brain

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:02.510
has special ways of handling faces and this is evident

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:04.089
through two lines of evidence.

00:15:04.089 --> 00:15:05.420
One line of evidence

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:06.790
is processing strategies.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:09.240
So behavioral studies that I'll talk about

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:12.620
show us that we seem to handle information in a face

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:13.130
differently

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:15.900
than we handle most other kinds of information.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:20.610
And also brain studies through, through

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:23.540
autopsies to intracranial recordings, through FMRI,

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.389
and through electrophysiological studies,

00:15:25.389 --> 00:15:27.370
on the outside of the scalp

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:32.330
we also find they're special brain regions to handle faces.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.410
In terms of processing strategies, two things

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:37.200
that are really robust findings

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.620
in faces are the inversion effect and decomposition effect.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:42.910
So the inversion effect is really simple,

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:45.210
when we turn a face upside down,

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:46.960
it's much harder to recognize.

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.839
We're all very good at recognizing faces.

00:15:49.839 --> 00:15:52.600
And it's pretty remarkable when you think about. Right, so
so

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.109
here's a room full of people

00:15:54.109 --> 00:15:56.680
and you all look completely distinct to me,

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:57.450
but it,

00:15:57.450 --> 00:15:59.410
but you also all have

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:03.830
two eyes, a nose, and a mouth. I apologize if I'm mistaken about anybody. I'm assuming.

00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:09.340
But you, you all have this same set of features on your face, right. We all are kind of the same

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.130
and yet we look totally different. If you think about carrots

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:13.430
or apples, right,

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:16.270
or monkeys, we have monkey researchers in the
room/

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:20.200
I have tried. I have done experiments where I use monkey faces,

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:24.330
monkey faces are at least as valuable as ours, right. They've got

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:25.330
different hair that might even 

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.010
make it a, a, better clue

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:28.310
about

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:30.520
telling monkeys apart, but I can't tell them apart.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:33.279
Right, we're really good at telling apart

00:16:33.279 --> 00:16:37.240
these things, these faces that are all pretty
similar,

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.550
but we turn them upside down and

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:40.810
we're not much better

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.310
at telling them apart than any, than anything else.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:46.620
Then there's also a finding called the decomposition
effect,

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:48.370
so even though

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:50.010
we can know

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.310
people's faces very well,

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:55.759
we're very bad at recognizing facial features

00:16:55.759 --> 00:16:57.740
outside of the context of a face.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.489
So even though this is my younger daughter, Aggie,

00:17:00.489 --> 00:17:03.970
so even though I recognize her face very well, you know,

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:09.040
pay, taking her eyes or her nose and putting them
outside the context of her face

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.300
with another baby's

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:11.510
nose or eyes

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:15.710
I'm not going do nearly as well as I would with the entire face.

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:17.929
And so what these kinds of findings, the idea that

00:17:17.929 --> 00:17:22.199
faces upside down or faces in pieces are
much harder to process,

00:17:22.199 --> 00:17:26.830
it tells us that we might be encoding the information
in the face in a different way.

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:28.650
So we think that most objects

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:31.020
we encode in a piecemeal way,

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:35.590
so kind of the idea that a monkey face or an apple or a carrot

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:38.180
is really just the sum of its parts,

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:39.580
but a human face isn't.

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:42.120
A human face is encoded as a whole using

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:45.510
configural processing strategy. So we learn faces

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.080
as a unit,

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:48.779
recognizing their features with respect

00:17:48.779 --> 00:17:50.440
to one another.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.539
So the idea is that this is a really distinct processing strategy

00:17:53.539 --> 00:17:57.040
that we apply selectively to human faces and that we,

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.600
we learn to use over time,

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:05.450
that as we become experts in faces we start to apply this processing strategy.

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:06.810
We also know

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.200
that we have special

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:10.380
parts of the brain to process faces.

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:14.940
And the first evidence for this came from
the work of a neurologist

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.290
and he found patients

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:19.360
who could lose the ability

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:21.710
to recognize individual faces,

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:22.630
while still being

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:24.950
fine at recognizing other things.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:26.940
So if they go to get their keys,

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:30.690
they're not going to pick up their co-worker's keys, but they go to pick up their wife, they might

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.150
pick-up their co-worker's wife.

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:37.580
So he, he, he looked at the brains of these people

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.230
and he found that

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:41.290
almost without variation

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:44.640
this disorder was secondary to

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.320
damage to certain parts of the cortex, part of the inferotemporal cortex.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.050
And most of the time

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:51.549
it was on the right,

00:18:51.549 --> 00:18:53.290
sometimes it could be on both, but most of the time

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:56.369
this was on the right. And so this was the first clue. Okay,
so maybe

00:18:56.369 --> 00:18:59.880
if people who get this part of the brain damaged

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.800
lose this skill,

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.100
maybe

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:05.150
this part of the brain has something to do

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:08.710
with the application of this skill.

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:13.170
And so now we have a lot more elegant ways to
look at the brains and you'll hear, I believe,

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:13.640
next week

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:17.820
from my colleague Kevin Pelphrey, who applies the method that's named on this slide,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:20.300
Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging.

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:24.039
And what Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging done, does, or FMRI,

00:19:24.039 --> 00:19:26.239
is lets us look at blood flow

00:19:26.239 --> 00:19:27.090
within the brain.

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:28.490
So it tells us,

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:32.170
when we have a person performing the task inside a FMRI magnet,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:35.320
we have a good chance sense of to where in their
brain blood is flowing

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:39.450
and that gives a very good sense of the spatial resolution
of activity,

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:44.230
so where in the brain things happen.

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:47.270
So, in 1997, a paper was published, a very

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:49.560
famous paper, a seminal work,

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:54.740
the third author of the paper some of you may recognize. He is now faculty member in the department of psychology,

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:55.940
Marvin Chun.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.830
And if I was allowed to show

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:02.280
copyrighted information you would see a picture
of Marvin from that journal article

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:05.690
with a little Fusiform Gyrus lighting up right next to him,

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:10.840
but we can't so we'll just say, so instead this is another excuse to put a picture of my daughter. There 

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.470
she's modeling

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:15.880
a neuro-scientist-in-training t-shirt

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:19.070
from a mentor of mine at,

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:22.350
at Harvard, Chuck Nelson, who has done a lot of pioneering work in,

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:23.780
in understanding

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:27.700
infant brain activity using ERP.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:32.809
So, what they found from using FMRI is if you show people faces compared to many, many things,

00:20:32.809 --> 00:20:34.930
animal faces, places,

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:35.880
hands,

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:40.830
you saw this part of the brain selectively activated, so it's the part of the brain called the Fusiform Gyrus,

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:44.020
which they call the Fusiform Face Area.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:47.650
There's a more nuanced story

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:49.340
and than, than just the Fusiform Face Area

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:51.990
that I'll get into in a little bit.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:55.730
But now let's talk a little bit about electrophysiology.

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:59.420
I think that electrophysiology is
a little mysterious than some of the other methods

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:00.550
that we use

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:01.750
to study,

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:03.540
to study brains, and to,

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:06.260
to look at people's behavior and things like
that. So

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:09.070
when we talk about electrophysiology, we often talk about ERP,

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:12.419
so ERP stands for Event-

00:21:12.419 --> 00:21:14.030
related Potential.

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:18.279
Have you all heard of EEG before?

00:21:18.279 --> 00:21:19.769
So maybe you've heard about it

00:21:19.769 --> 00:21:23.650
because EEGs are used to clinically, so

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:26.880
EEG is electroencephalaogram and that's just a recording of the electrical activity 

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:28.039
that your brain produces.

00:21:28.039 --> 00:21:31.090
So all the time when we're doing anything,

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:32.840
as long as we're alive

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:37.700
our brains are producing a small amount of
electricity.

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:41.250
And it's noisy, lots of different things
are happening in that electricity.

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:45.040
If I just show you a face and look at the electricity your brain is making,

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:46.730
it's going to be kind of confusing,

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:48.760
and if I show you a second face,

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:53.760
it might not look exactly like what we saw in
response to the first face,

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.150
but what we can do

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:59.330
is if we show you many, many faces,

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:00.180
eventually

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:04.710
what is the electrical activity that is
elicited specifically

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:06.260
by that face

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:09.080
is going to kind of emerge from the noise, right.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:12.909
So if I do the same thing many times, sixty times, a hundred times,

00:22:12.909 --> 00:22:16.230
eventually if I collapse all of those trials together and I average them,

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:18.720
I'm going to get a decent enough to noise ratio

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.850
that I will see the electrical truth.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.719
I'll see the activity

00:22:22.719 --> 00:22:25.489
that is happening in response to this discrete event,

00:22:25.489 --> 00:22:26.570
this happening

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:30.420
that is related to this event, this event-related potential.

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:31.960
ERPs

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:35.540
don't reflect the refiring, the firing of a
single neuron,

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:40.190
they reflect the firing of many, many neurons that are firing in synchrony.

00:22:40.190 --> 00:22:43.380
So it really gives us a window and a particular
time

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.090
of brain activit.y

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:49.640
The nice thing about ERPs is their timing.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:52.370
So I, I stressed before

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:53.179
with FMRI 

00:22:53.179 --> 00:22:58.150
that we get a very good sense of where in
the brain things happens,

00:22:58.150 --> 00:22:59.770
with an ERP we don't.

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:04.149
I think about ERPs almost like shadows, electrical shadows 

00:23:04.149 --> 00:23:05.130
on the scalp.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:05.809
And so

00:23:05.809 --> 00:23:07.840
you know that a shadow has been cast

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:12.650
and you can show two different kinds of images
and see whether the shadows are the same or different,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:15.040
but you really don't know exactly

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:20.450
where the light source is creating that shadow
or what exactly the size of the object creating that shadow is.

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:24.980
So we have a very difficult time knowing exactly
where in the brain ERPs come from,

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:26.020
but we can

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:29.950
really pinpoint in a way that we can't with other neuro-imaging methods

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:31.430
the timing.

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:35.950
So ERPs gives us millisecond resolution to understand

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:36.950
differences

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:39.110
in cognitive events in the brain.

00:23:39.110 --> 00:23:44.130
So the, the resolution of ERPs parallels
the resolution of actual brain processes.

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:45.200
So we can get a sense

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:47.509
of things like the timing of a response

00:23:47.509 --> 00:23:49.490
that we can't get from other methods.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:52.000
Now when I say that we, 

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:56.100
we can't really tell where in the brain things are coming from with ERP,

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:56.659
that's

00:23:56.659 --> 00:23:58.570
kind of a debated point.

00:23:58.570 --> 00:24:01.000
That's certainly the conservative stance to take.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.960
As an ERP researcher, maybe I should come out

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:04.920
kind of fighting a little bit

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:05.510
and take a,

00:24:05.510 --> 00:24:09.870
a more aggressive stance because what we can do is a process called source localization,

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.389
so if I have many, many points,

00:24:12.389 --> 00:24:14.480
data points on the scalp

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:15.700
and I can

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:17.470
measure the electrical activity of all of them,

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:19.500
I can kind of test hypotheses.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.380
I can say well if I see

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:24.380
a pattern of activity that is happening in response
to a face,

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:27.840
I know for FMRI studies that Fusiform Gyrus is involved in faces,

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:31.180
so I can kind of create a seed in that Fusiform Gyrus 

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:32.179
and see

00:24:32.179 --> 00:24:34.580
if electricity, if electrical activity

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:35.600
propagating from there

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:37.480
could potentially result

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:40.320
in the pattern of activity I see on the scalp.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:41.470
So we can do that

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:43.320
and that's called source localization analysis

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.110
and I'll actually show you an example of
that later today.

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:47.309
The problem is,

00:24:47.309 --> 00:24:48.950
is the inverse problem,

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:50.890
and the inverse problem is really simply that

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:55.049
for any given, because there could be more
than one source of electrical activity in

00:24:55.049 --> 00:24:55.630
the brain,

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:57.559
for any given scalp distribution,

00:24:57.559 --> 00:24:58.950
there could be an infinite number of 

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:01.650
potential solutions inside the brain.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.060
So, we can get a sense of where things come from

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:04.940
with a,

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:07.120
with ERP where things come from in the brain, 

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.530
but it's really not

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:10.990
as, as robust as FMRI.

00:25:10.990 --> 00:25:14.909
And really we're increasing starting to think
about them as complementary tools. Now we have

00:25:14.909 --> 00:25:16.090
the technology

00:25:16.090 --> 00:25:19.570
to be able to record ERPs inside FMRI magnets, 

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:21.809
so that we can look at both the timing

00:25:21.809 --> 00:25:22.960
and the,

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.610
and the places in the brain

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:25.760
where things happen.

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:28.679
So what does an ERP look like?

00:25:28.679 --> 00:25:30.280
So this is a, a

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:31.929
Geodesics sensor net.

00:25:31.929 --> 00:25:33.160
Im going to 

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:35.040
give lots of attention

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:36.330
to the creators

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:39.440
of this net because I'm using pictures from their
website.

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:44.490
So this is electrical Geodesics. The director John Tupper is a close collaborator

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:45.520
of ours.

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:46.840
This is actually an updated

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:49.870
version that they sent me several years ago,

00:25:49.870 --> 00:25:55.510
but basically the idea is you have many electrodes. This is a two hundred and fifty six

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:56.650
electrode net.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:59.270
And so you see

00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:04.190
you could, all of those white things that
you see are plastic pedestals.

00:26:04.190 --> 00:26:06.660
Inside each one of those plastic pedestals,

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:08.730
and you can kind of see it right

00:26:08.730 --> 00:26:09.730
there,

00:26:09.730 --> 00:26:13.000
is a small sponge.

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.029
Inside that sponge is a small electrical censor,

00:26:16.029 --> 00:26:18.229
which is connected by all of these wires

00:26:18.229 --> 00:26:21.830
to an amplifier to boast the signal because it's a really

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.730
small signal, that's why we don't shock

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:24.620
each other.

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:27.640
And then that amplifier feeds to a computer that records

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:29.910
the brain activity.

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:31.300
The reason, who can guess,

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:33.809
maybe it's not a guess if you've been involved with an EEG,

00:26:33.809 --> 00:26:36.009
why would we want to have sponges in

00:26:36.009 --> 00:26:37.350
there?

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:40.320
And I'll give you a clue. Why don't you

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:47.320
wade in the surf during a lightning storm?

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:49.049
Exactly right.

00:26:49.049 --> 00:26:53.510
And what kind of water best conducts electricity?

00:26:53.510 --> 00:26:59.700
I'll give you another hint. We might, we would wade in a lake before we waded in the ocean in a lightning storm. Exactly.

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:06.300
So we actually, so we soak all of these sponges in an electrolite solution. It's actually potassium chloride not sodium

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:07.040
chloride.

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:08.320
And that helps

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:09.919
pick up the electricity from the scalp.

00:27:09.919 --> 00:27:12.090
So this is a really nice technology.

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:14.609
There's different ways to record EEG.

00:27:14.609 --> 00:27:16.659
There's the traditional way

00:27:16.659 --> 00:27:23.059
is to kind of scratch the scalp a little bit with like a needle and use a, an abrasive gel that kind of creates the contact. But

00:27:23.059 --> 00:27:25.679
this is a really nice solution,

00:27:25.679 --> 00:27:26.349
especially

00:27:26.349 --> 00:27:27.899
if you're working with

00:27:27.899 --> 00:27:28.880
babies

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:31.130
or has anybody, anybody familiar with

00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:33.499
the developmental disability

00:27:33.499 --> 00:27:37.760
sometimes associated with sensory sensitivities?

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:41.850
Like throughout, let's say hypothetically you're sitting in a class about autism, right.

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:45.450
So it's a nicer solution for working with people with autism because it's a little bit easier to 

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:45.839
tolerate

00:27:45.839 --> 00:27:47.670
than scratching someone's scalp

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.710
and using the gel base?

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:53.330
The way you apply it is you just kind of stretch it
over a person's head.

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:54.580
And here's a,

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:55.520
a close up

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:59.650
of what those electrodes look like and this
is from the newer version from what we

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:00.740
call

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:03.720
hydro cell net, which just have smaller sponges, sponges embedded

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:07.900
in little cups that actually help things not dry out so fast, so

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:09.130
so you can do longer experiments.

00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:10.750
So what's,

00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:11.789
any questions about

00:28:11.789 --> 00:28:18.789
the technique or this hat or any of this stuff or any questions about anything I've talked about so far?

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:36.040
Well how, you mean, how do you the experiment?

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:38.159
It's a really good question.

00:28:38.159 --> 00:28:40.000
Another woman? And to, well let me

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:42.330
compound your question with a question.

00:28:42.330 --> 00:28:43.260
Professor McPartland,

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:47.020
how do they do it when infants have such poor visual
acuity?

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:50.570
And the answer is they probably do it with configuration, so

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:53.919
infants see low spacial frequencies better, so they're going to kind of see the dark spots.

00:28:53.919 --> 00:28:54.650
And so

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:58.020
that's even remarkable, right, it's not like they're saying my mom's the one 

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.860
with the really salient crow's feet.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:06.860
No, they're recognizing my mom's the one with
this configuration of eyes, nose, and mouth. My question is when do they encode the face? So obviously they have a test phase in this study? So do they encode it? You know, when do they encode the face before this time?

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:16.220
It's a good question.

00:29:17.650 --> 00:29:18.929
We couldn't be sure, right. We,

00:29:18.929 --> 00:29:22.840
my guess would be, and this is coming both

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:24.659
less from being a, a 

00:29:24.659 --> 00:29:28.820
cognitive neuro-scientist, a child psychologist, a

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:30.009
person who studies human development,

00:29:30.009 --> 00:29:31.580
and more from a father,

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:33.760
is that when a baby is born

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:36.119
and you put them to the breast to nurse

00:29:36.119 --> 00:29:38.200
or even if you don't breast feed, you give them bottle

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:39.230
that infant gazes

00:29:39.230 --> 00:29:40.590
so intently

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:42.040
at a person's face

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:43.600
that, that's probably how.

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:45.750
And it's really,

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:50.390
I don't, I don't know the different theories
about what exactly, exactly happens, is happening

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:53.580
in the brains of babies in the moments when they are born, but

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:56.140
little babies are,

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:57.500
can be quite ornery,

00:29:57.500 --> 00:29:58.640
but they're generally

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:00.740
pretty peaceful. It's this period 

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:02.330
of kind of they call it, what do they,

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:03.879
wakeful alertness

00:30:03.879 --> 00:30:05.139
right after infants are born,

00:30:05.139 --> 00:30:08.720
when they kind of look at everything and take everything in, so who knows, maybe they're.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.440
But it's a good question, but it's not,

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:11.620
it's not known.

00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:12.460
We could look at it,

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:18.870
I mean, we can use ERPs on very very early babies so who knows, I mean, you could look at 

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:20.650
response to a

00:30:20.650 --> 00:30:23.690
what's happen, what's happening and I guess
it would be pretty hard to look at

00:30:23.690 --> 00:30:24.860
because you need multiple trials,

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:26.649
but we could record their response rates per.

00:30:26.649 --> 00:30:28.960
It could also be some kind of pairing right,

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:32.950
so even though an infant's seeing his or her mother's face for the very first time, time, they've been hearing

00:30:32.950 --> 00:30:34.300
the voice

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:35.560
for a long time, right.

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:37.600
And so maybe it's hearing,

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:40.380
seeing this come with this familiar voice.

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:41.420
But, it's a good question.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:45.820
Any other questions? I'm so happy we got a question, right, mid-lecture. This is banner day

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:47.860
for psychology 350.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:51.900
Any other questions, okay.

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:56.650
So the nice thing about ERPs is really all you have to be able to do is

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:58.980
to be able tolerate wearing a hat.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.980
So we can put this hat on very, very young
babies, and

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:04.070
infants

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:06.200
children, adults.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:09.239
When working with people with autism, we can put this hat on people with autism,

00:31:09.239 --> 00:31:11.109
who are much, much smarter

00:31:11.109 --> 00:31:12.430
than anybody in this room.

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:14.260
We can also put this on people with 

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:14.969
autism

00:31:14.969 --> 00:31:17.279
who don't have any language

00:31:17.279 --> 00:31:19.179
or who have ostensible cognitive impairments.

00:31:19.179 --> 00:31:20.809
So it really gives us a,

00:31:20.809 --> 00:31:25.770
it's widely applicable in terms of studying brain function.

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:30.760
So when we think about the way we do an ERP is we have a person wear this hat 

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:33.800
they sit in a chair in front of a, a TV monitor.

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:36.620
It doesn't have to be a TV monitor, we a,

00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:39.350
you can also do ERPs to auditory stimuli.  

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.720
In fact people have actually

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:43.040
to try to, so

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:45.039
I'll talk about face perception today

00:31:45.039 --> 00:31:48.409
and all the faces I show you are, they're not real faces.

00:31:48.409 --> 00:31:51.980
They're pictures of faces on a computer screen. So people have reasoned,

00:31:51.980 --> 00:31:55.509
well, how can we be sure that people will treat the face on the computer screen the same way

00:31:55.509 --> 00:31:58.980
as a regular face. People have actually done experiments where they have a,

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:00.840
a person behind, what's it

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:02.259
called, piezoelectric 

00:32:02.259 --> 00:32:03.200
piece of glass,

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.960
so when you turn charge, you can make the glass opaque or transparent.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:09.120
So they've actually done ERPs to real faces, but

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:10.840
here we do ERPs

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:12.390
to, to pictures on a computer screen.

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:16.460
You make the same thing happen many, many, many times.

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:19.040
One thing that's really important about ERPs

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:21.620
is making sure that you have some measure

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:23.670
of a person's attention

00:32:23.670 --> 00:32:25.800
because what if,

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:27.350
you know, I do a study

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:30.100
of face processing in people with autism 

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:31.889
and typically developing peers

00:32:31.889 --> 00:32:33.950
and I see differences, but

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:36.900
what if the people with autism were all looking out the window

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:40.250
during the course of the experiment, right. That could be the reason for different brain activity.

00:32:40.250 --> 00:32:42.590
And so it's a very important you have some kind
of measure

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:44.490
that person is paying attention.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.840
And so these are, these are images taken from
the website

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:52.590
of the Locke lab led by Steven Locke, who's really one of the pioneers 

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:53.840
of ERP,

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.430
if any of you, of you are interested 

00:32:55.430 --> 00:32:59.300
in, in reading more about the basis of ERP, I'd be happy to lend you

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:02.380
his very excellent introductory books.

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:06.340
What you can see is you have sort of ongoing EEG

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:08.620
and then we have things that happen.

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:12.950
So if you see here this is the first, let's say, you know, he just calls it stimulus, but let's say it's the first 

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:13.610
face

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:15.640
and then we see a response

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:18.520
and then the second face and then we see a
response.

00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:20.139
And then so on

00:33:20.139 --> 00:33:21.110
to the nth face. 

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:22.889
And we see lots of different responses

00:33:22.889 --> 00:33:24.129
and then what we can do

00:33:24.129 --> 00:33:26.760
is take all of those responses

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:28.799
and average them together. So 

00:33:28.799 --> 00:33:31.209
if you see this one, this one, this one,

00:33:31.209 --> 00:33:35.419
they have some commonalities, right. For all of
them have some kind of

00:33:35.419 --> 00:33:36.820
dip here.

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:39.760
There's something else over here

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:41.480
and then there's something else over here.

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:43.610
But when we average them

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:45.770
all the different kinds of pieces of noise

00:33:45.770 --> 00:33:50.210
because lots of things are happening. You might be
blinking. You might be scratching your leg. You might be

00:33:50.210 --> 00:33:52.470
thinking about your homework assignments or

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:54.019
what your going to do this weekend,

00:33:54.019 --> 00:33:56.960
but you're not going to be doing all of those things

00:33:56.960 --> 00:33:58.009
in perfect synchrony

00:33:58.009 --> 00:34:02.159
with the presentation of each face each time and so when we only look at the activity that's in perfect synchrony with the

00:34:02.159 --> 00:34:03.940
face,

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:06.160
we eventually get an ERP wave form,

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:07.740
which looks like this.

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:09.160
So it's a little bit smoother

00:34:09.160 --> 00:34:12.159
and yet it preserves the kind of

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:15.559
features of each individual trial.

00:34:15.559 --> 00:34:17.969
When we think about ERPs

00:34:17.969 --> 00:34:22.039
there's, this is the, the most basic way to analyze
them. There's a

00:34:22.039 --> 00:34:27.519
now more sophisticated ways using multivariant statistics and I'm not going to talk about today, but

00:34:27.519 --> 00:34:30.770
we can think about ERP components
and an ERP component

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:33.500
is really just the feature of an ERP wave form

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:36.630
that we reason to be linked to some kind of
cognitive event.

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:40.149
And the two aspects of an ERP component that we pay attention to are

00:34:40.149 --> 00:34:41.740
the amplitude,

00:34:41.740 --> 00:34:43.429
which is usually expressed in micro-volts,

00:34:43.429 --> 00:34:46.369
and that's shown by that purple arrow there,

00:34:46.369 --> 00:34:47.649
and the latency,

00:34:47.649 --> 00:34:50.439
which is expressed in milliseconds, and that's show by that,

00:34:50.439 --> 00:34:51.859
that green arrow there.

00:34:51.859 --> 00:34:54.619
So this would be a component

00:34:54.619 --> 00:34:57.669
and we would talk about its amplitude is how

00:34:57.669 --> 00:35:00.249
far down it's going and its latency is when it peaks.

00:35:00.249 --> 00:35:01.709
When we talk about ERP components 

00:35:01.709 --> 00:35:03.820
the way we refer to them

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:07.429
is usually with a combination of one letter

00:35:07.429 --> 00:35:10.999
and a, a, a number or series of numbers and there are,

00:35:10.999 --> 00:35:13.370
for certain exceptions to this rule.

00:35:13.370 --> 00:35:16.369
But generally speaking we talk about things
being N or P,

00:35:16.369 --> 00:35:17.980
N meaning it's a negative

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:21.999
going electrical component, P meaning it's a positive electrical going component.

00:35:21.999 --> 00:35:26.269
And then we can call it either an, like an N1 or an N2

00:35:26.269 --> 00:35:27.980
reflects an ordinate value.

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:32.290
So an N1 would be the first negative peak in a wave form. N2 would be the second

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:33.479
negative peak in a wave form.

00:35:33.479 --> 00:35:35.650
We can also refer to it specifically 

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:37.260
with the timing of the peak

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.500
and that would be, for example, we'll talk about
today

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:40.990
and N170.

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:47.599
So N170 would a component that goes positive or negative?

00:35:47.599 --> 00:35:49.559
Come on, this is an easy one guys.

00:35:49.559 --> 00:35:50.509
That's right, negative.

00:35:50.509 --> 00:35:54.799
And what will be the latency of N170?

00:35:54.799 --> 00:36:00.049
How does that, so N, so is a hundred seventy milliseconds fast or slow? That's fast.

00:36:00.049 --> 00:36:04.269
A hundred seventy milliseconds is not a long time, right. So that's one of the neat things 

00:36:04.269 --> 00:36:06.879
about ERP. And that's one of the reasons we're going to talk about  

00:36:06.879 --> 00:36:08.249
the N170 because it's really, it,

00:36:08.249 --> 00:36:11.780
the first study that kind of talked about N170 

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:14.289
was in 1996.

00:36:14.289 --> 00:36:16.299
One of my favorite studies of all time 

00:36:16.299 --> 00:36:18.050
by a guy named Shlomo Bentin,

00:36:18.050 --> 00:36:21.539
who I believe actually did a post-doc here at Yale 

00:36:21.539 --> 00:36:23.089
earlier in his career.

00:36:23.089 --> 00:36:26.370
And he found that when you show people lots of different
kinds of things

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:29.740
you've got this negative spike at a hundred and seventy milliseconds

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:31.550
that was largest and

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:32.019
fastest

00:36:32.019 --> 00:36:33.030
to faces.

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:34.630
It was a really robust finding.

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:39.219
And what was so interesting about this to people, cause people,

00:36:39.219 --> 00:36:42.439
this had been looked at in other ways, but

00:36:42.439 --> 00:36:43.860
what was really interesting

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:46.950
was to see that this happens so fast, 

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:48.690
that a hundred and seventy milliseconds

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:50.079
after seeing a face 

00:36:50.079 --> 00:36:52.200
your brain is treating that

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:53.529
differently from everything else.

00:36:53.529 --> 00:36:55.239
So that was kind of news.

00:36:55.239 --> 00:36:59.749
So now since then there have been many,
many, many studies

00:36:59.749 --> 00:37:01.649
looking at the N170,

00:37:01.649 --> 00:37:04.170
all kinds of manipulations.

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:08.140
What happens when you turn faces upside down? What happens when you do a, a

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:11.740
photographic inversion of a face, so it looks like a photonegative? 

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:15.790
What does the N170 look like in little kids? What does it look like in people with autism? What does it look like in 

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:17.220
people with schizophrenia? So

00:37:17.220 --> 00:37:18.980
there's been many, many studies

00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:20.179
of the N170.

00:37:20.179 --> 00:37:22.579
Some of the basic things to know about it

00:37:22.579 --> 00:37:24.300
are that it represents the, the

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:26.730
earliest stages of face processing,

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:30.469
maybe not not the earliest stage. There's some evidence
that

00:37:30.469 --> 00:37:34.910
activity at a hundred milliseconds might also
individuate faces from other things or

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:37.439
distinguish faces from other kinds of individual stimuli.

00:37:37.439 --> 00:37:40.339
We think that it represents structural encoding.

00:37:40.339 --> 00:37:45.529
So this is the stage of face perception in which your brain registers a face as a face, 

00:37:45.529 --> 00:37:46.640
so not

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:52.839
this is my mother or this is my best friend or this is my
neighbor, but just this is a face.

00:37:52.839 --> 00:37:55.769
The N170 is sensitive to inversion.

00:37:55.769 --> 00:37:59.359
Well, this is kind of neat. Remember when we talked about processing strategies and what happens when 

00:37:59.359 --> 00:38:02.200
we turn a face upside down.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:02.800
Easier or harder to recognize?

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:05.899
I'm going to keep you all

00:38:05.899 --> 00:38:08.079
awake. Harder to recognize.

00:38:08.079 --> 00:38:10.309
So we a,

00:38:10.309 --> 00:38:14.399
we might think, so if you were going to make a guess
about what would happen to a face-specific component, so

00:38:14.399 --> 00:38:16.050
we turn faces upside down, we stop

00:38:16.050 --> 00:38:18.349
treating them like faces,

00:38:18.349 --> 00:38:19.730
we turn faces upside down,

00:38:19.730 --> 00:38:25.539
what should happen to a face-specific ERP component?

00:38:25.539 --> 00:38:26.909
It should go away, right?

00:38:26.909 --> 00:38:30.019
We should, we would, we wouldn't treat it as a face. Exact opposite. 

00:38:30.019 --> 00:38:30.990
It actually gets bigger

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:32.400
and it gets slower.

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:34.299
And so that's called the inversion effect.

00:38:34.299 --> 00:38:36.589
And so I mentioned that we can study

00:38:36.589 --> 00:38:38.340
ERPs in very young children.

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:42.610
People have looked at face perceptions in very young children. So by three months

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:44.330
we see specific activity

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:45.720
elicited by faces.

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:47.060
It happens later.

00:38:47.060 --> 00:38:48.980
So it's kind of a, an amalgamation of

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:50.880
activity distributed between

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:52.400
an N290

00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:54.569
and a P400.

00:38:54.569 --> 00:38:56.499
And then over time those seems to

00:38:56.499 --> 00:38:58.380
smear together. Both get faster.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:02.450
And we have the adult-like patterns by the
time a person is around fourteen years.

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:03.539
So this is a,

00:39:03.539 --> 00:39:07.330
a face-specific component or, I should say, a face-sensitive
component,

00:39:07.330 --> 00:39:08.559
but we can also

00:39:08.559 --> 00:39:09.829
just look at

00:39:09.829 --> 00:39:11.829
other brain components

00:39:11.829 --> 00:39:14.810
and put faces into a paradigm in which other
things could be.

00:39:14.810 --> 00:39:16.979
So we could look at recognition memory

00:39:16.979 --> 00:39:17.670
for faces.

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:18.889
We could look at

00:39:18.889 --> 00:39:20.430
the effect of salience

00:39:20.430 --> 00:39:24.149
for faces all I'll talk about all those different kinds of things 

00:39:24.149 --> 00:39:24.590
in some,

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:28.029
in some of the ERP studies of autism that I'm going to talk about.

00:39:28.029 --> 00:39:29.690
So let's move on and talk a little bit

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:32.140
more about autism. What do we know about faces

00:39:32.140 --> 00:39:33.160
in autism?

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.479
And I going to make a disclaimer right at the start,

00:39:35.479 --> 00:39:36.259
faces are

00:39:36.259 --> 00:39:37.119
now

00:39:37.119 --> 00:39:40.829
one of the most well studied,

00:39:40.829 --> 00:39:46.519
one of the most well studied areas of research in autism and I guess well studied,

00:39:46.519 --> 00:39:50.310
well studied gives an inappropriate connotation, cause well, well studied

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:51.749
seems to suggest that,

00:39:51.749 --> 00:39:53.280
that they're well understood

00:39:53.280 --> 00:39:54.760
and it's the exact opposite.

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:57.400
There's been so much research in faces,

00:39:57.400 --> 00:40:00.249
but the research is really

00:40:00.249 --> 00:40:01.990
painted a confusing picture.

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:03.560
And so I'm going to give you

00:40:03.560 --> 00:40:07.809
a story they can fit into one class today,

00:40:07.809 --> 00:40:10.280
but, but it's a more nuanced story. And I'll actually

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:13.519
tell you about some of the work that we're doing to shed light on those nuances at the very end of my lecture

00:40:13.519 --> 00:40:16.289
cause that's one of our,

00:40:16.289 --> 00:40:18.090
our, our lab's goals now

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:23.459
is to try and understand well, why does some studies find certain problems with faces and other studies don't?

00:40:23.459 --> 00:40:24.499
Why,

00:40:24.499 --> 00:40:26.290
why do we see this heterogeneity 

00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:28.589
in face perception in autism?

00:40:28.589 --> 00:40:31.989
And it's worth mentioning, it's worth mentioning

00:40:31.989 --> 00:40:33.740
one of Dr. Clinton's first slides

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:36.249
showed the idea of an autism spectrum, right,

00:40:36.249 --> 00:40:40.159
so there were lots of people, all different
colors, under a rainbow. It looked like

00:40:40.159 --> 00:40:42.959
some kind of happy autism party.

00:40:42.959 --> 00:40:46.180
Was it, if we think about the that we diagnose autism, right,

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:48.570
there's a list of twelve symptoms.

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:53.920
How many symptoms do you need to get a diagnosis
of autism, not even autism spectrum disorder, autism,

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:56.150
the most severe diagnosis on the spectrum?

00:40:56.150 --> 00:40:58.469
Does anybody know?

00:40:58.469 --> 00:40:59.969
Six. Six.

00:40:59.969 --> 00:41:01.289
So, six of twelve.

00:41:01.289 --> 00:41:05.819
So you could theoretically have two children diagnosed with autism
with non-overlapping symptoms.

00:41:05.819 --> 00:41:10.450
So it makes abs, to mean, absolutely perfect sense that we would have heterogeneity

00:41:10.450 --> 00:41:11.680
in research studies

00:41:11.680 --> 00:41:14.919
of people with autism.

00:41:14.919 --> 00:41:19.289
So one of the really most robust findings in autism both clinically, and I'm, I'm sure

00:41:19.289 --> 00:41:21.979
many of you have noticed this now in your practicum places

00:41:21.979 --> 00:41:24.830
is that people with autism seem to approach faces

00:41:24.830 --> 00:41:28.129
in a different way.

00:41:28.129 --> 00:41:30.950
Before we had these really elegant studies

00:41:30.950 --> 00:41:33.409
that we have now, where we're taking infants

00:41:33.409 --> 00:41:35.160
who are at risk for autism

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:37.630
by virtue of having an older sibling with autism,

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:39.709
and studying the development of the disorder 

00:41:39.709 --> 00:41:41.490
in a prospective way,

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:44.690
we use to study it in retrospective ways.

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:46.080
So one way

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:49.509
to study things retrospectively is using video tapes.

00:41:49.509 --> 00:41:51.309
And so my graduate advisor,

00:41:51.309 --> 00:41:53.729
Geraldine Dawson, and a person who was a graduate
student

00:41:53.729 --> 00:41:56.249
at the time, Julie Osterling,

00:41:56.249 --> 00:41:57.359
had this idea

00:41:57.359 --> 00:41:59.370
to look at first birthday parties.

00:41:59.370 --> 00:42:01.349
So it's a nice experiment, right, but

00:42:01.349 --> 00:42:05.909
what do we want in an experiment? We want things to be well controlled, standardized as the same for everybody,

00:42:05.909 --> 00:42:08.450
and we can't really do that with home videos,

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:11.770
but for birthday parties the same kinds
of things

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:14.649
happen at every birthday party, so

00:42:14.649 --> 00:42:16.600
we have a nice comparison to see how infants might be different.

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:20.730
And also when we're interested in things like social referencing, social referencing is the idea

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:23.920
that you look to a, another person's face to figure out

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:25.709
what to make sense of some, what,

00:42:25.709 --> 00:42:28.089
how to make sense of something novel,

00:42:28.089 --> 00:42:29.010
and so

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:30.769
what better situation

00:42:30.769 --> 00:42:33.700
to elicit social referencing in a one-year-old
child

00:42:33.700 --> 00:42:35.910
than for the first time in their life to take
out

00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:37.479
a, a piece of dessert,

00:42:37.479 --> 00:42:38.660
put it in front of them,

00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:41.129
and then set it on fire, right.

00:42:41.129 --> 00:42:43.460
Most kids have never had parents set their, their

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:44.189
highchair

00:42:44.189 --> 00:42:45.559
on fire before.

00:42:45.559 --> 00:42:48.779
And so it's a nice chance for students, for, for kids to try to figure out

00:42:48.779 --> 00:42:50.569
is this something good, is this something bad.

00:42:50.569 --> 00:42:52.920
And so what they did is that they looked at these
home videos

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:54.169
and they found that

00:42:54.169 --> 00:42:57.829
first birthdays, and then in subsequent studies, even earlier,

00:42:57.829 --> 00:42:59.729
by six months,

00:42:59.729 --> 00:43:02.229
kids who would go on to be diagnosed with autism

00:43:02.229 --> 00:43:04.109
look different from kids

00:43:04.109 --> 00:43:05.640
who went on to develop typically

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:07.539
and also kids who went on to have

00:43:07.539 --> 00:43:10.359
non-autistic developmental delays.

00:43:10.359 --> 00:43:11.670
We know

00:43:11.670 --> 00:43:15.119
that throughout the lifespan, people with autism have more trouble 

00:43:15.119 --> 00:43:17.289
with recognizing faces,

00:43:17.289 --> 00:43:19.920
with interpreting emotional expressions,

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:22.270
with maintaining eye gaze.

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:24.179
That it's really striking,

00:43:24.179 --> 00:43:29.419
I have encountered striking anecdotal examples. A fellow I worked with at the University of Washington

00:43:29.419 --> 00:43:30.009
was a,

00:43:30.009 --> 00:43:32.389
a man with high functioning autism

00:43:32.389 --> 00:43:33.819
and

00:43:33.819 --> 00:43:36.199
he's interest, his preoccupation

00:43:36.199 --> 00:43:38.890
was food choices. And so in the halls

00:43:38.890 --> 00:43:41.459
of the building he would approach me and say 

00:43:41.459 --> 00:43:43.079
Jamie, do you, do you

00:43:43.079 --> 00:43:45.200
like apple pie or do you like

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:46.960
pumpkin pie?

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:52.239
And then I would say I like, I like both and he said well, do you like them or do you love them. And he would kind of ask this series of questions to

00:43:52.239 --> 00:43:53.180
really get at your,

00:43:53.180 --> 00:43:54.660
your food preferences,

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:57.729
but when I would see him outside

00:43:57.729 --> 00:43:58.650
of work

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:00.409
I'd walk past him on the street

00:44:00.409 --> 00:44:02.750
he wouldn't recognize me, he wouldn't say anything, but then if I'd say 

00:44:02.750 --> 00:44:05.119
hey how're you doing, he would say

00:44:05.119 --> 00:44:07.699
do you like apples or do you love them?

00:44:07.699 --> 00:44:08.849
And so he,

00:44:08.849 --> 00:44:12.269
he needed a context for my voice to recognize me.

00:44:12.269 --> 00:44:13.720
We also see different

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:16.229
processing strategies for faces in autism.

00:44:16.229 --> 00:44:20.739
We see a reduced inversion effect, so they don't have the same decrement in performances when

00:44:20.739 --> 00:44:23.109
we turn faces upside down.

00:44:23.109 --> 00:44:27.110
We don't see the same decomposition effect,
in fact, some studies have shown

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:28.360
that they're better

00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:30.510
at featured-based matching, especially

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:32.049
if it revolves around the mouth.

00:44:32.049 --> 00:44:36.899
And so we infer that people with autism aren't using this configural processing strategy that we've

00:44:36.899 --> 00:44:37.749
talked about.

00:44:37.749 --> 00:44:40.249
They're approaching faces in a piecemeal way,

00:44:40.249 --> 00:44:43.189
the way most of us approach objects.

00:44:43.189 --> 00:44:46.169
You've heard someone speak much more eloquently

00:44:46.169 --> 00:44:49.499
than I would be able to about this already,

00:44:49.499 --> 00:44:52.469
but Dr. Clinton and Warren Jones and others

00:44:52.469 --> 00:44:54.450
have looked at the way people with autism 

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:57.150
look at faces and look at social information.

00:44:57.150 --> 00:45:02.849
And we know that people with autism look less at the eyes. They look more at mouths and

00:45:02.849 --> 00:45:03.519
things.

00:45:03.519 --> 00:45:06.709
When there's three people interacting on screen,

00:45:06.709 --> 00:45:08.959
they have a harder time tracking

00:45:08.959 --> 00:45:10.380
those interactions.

00:45:10.380 --> 00:45:15.000
And they, they fail to follow attentional cues, things like a person pointing their finger

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:16.859
on screen.

00:45:16.859 --> 00:45:19.599
From FMRI research,

00:45:19.599 --> 00:45:24.119
that actually happened here. Bob Schultz, another close colleague who has since moved on to

00:45:24.119 --> 00:45:29.079
the children's hospital of Philadelphia to start the center for autism research there to produce another seminal paper

00:45:29.079 --> 00:45:31.469
at the child's study center 

00:45:31.469 --> 00:45:32.770
looking at

00:45:32.770 --> 00:45:35.119
this activity in the Fusiform face area.

00:45:35.119 --> 00:45:39.039
So we've talked about how Marvin Chun and Nancy Kin, Kanwisher

00:45:39.039 --> 00:45:43.640
found this hot spot in the brain for faces, well Bob looked at people with autism

00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:45.970
and saw how their brains activated to faces

00:45:45.970 --> 00:45:50.079
and he saw that this activity was, was reduced in people with autism,

00:45:50.079 --> 00:45:53.349
despite activity to objects looking very similar.

00:45:53.349 --> 00:45:54.699
So now let's talk 

00:45:54.699 --> 00:45:55.959
a little bit

00:45:55.959 --> 00:45:58.019
about ERP studies

00:45:58.019 --> 00:46:00.170
of face-related brain function in autism.

00:46:00.170 --> 00:46:02.840
so we're going to talk about three different areas,

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:05.559
so face recognition,

00:46:05.559 --> 00:46:07.160
emotion recognition,

00:46:07.160 --> 00:46:08.989
and structural encoding.

00:46:08.989 --> 00:46:12.269
And so first I'm going to talk about face recognition

00:46:12.269 --> 00:46:14.630
and this is work we that we did at the University of Washington

00:46:14.630 --> 00:46:18.360
as part of a study of early development of
children with autism.

00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:20.609
We did this work in

00:46:20.609 --> 00:46:24.019
three-year-olds and this was actually based very closely on a paradigm used in typical

00:46:24.019 --> 00:46:25.969
development, a paradigm 

00:46:25.969 --> 00:46:27.269
created by Chuck Nelson.

00:46:27.269 --> 00:46:28.579
And so what we did

00:46:28.579 --> 00:46:32.160
is we showed children familiar and unfamiliar faces

00:46:32.160 --> 00:46:33.749
and this is the slide 

00:46:33.749 --> 00:46:38.599
where someday when my daughters are old enough to log on to Youtube,

00:46:38.599 --> 00:46:40.389
they can still think

00:46:40.389 --> 00:46:40.980
that I'm a jerk,

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:44.289
but at least they'll know I wasn't a hypocrite.

00:46:44.289 --> 00:46:47.420
So we, we showed them familiar and unfamiliar faces

00:46:47.420 --> 00:46:49.830
and familiar and unfamiliar toys.

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:52.060
And we contrasted brain activity. So

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:54.030
what's the difference between a face and a toy

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:57.519
in terms of, think about the developmental paths I talked about earlier,

00:46:57.519 --> 00:47:02.999
which path is for faces?

00:47:02.999 --> 00:47:06.179
The social path or the non-social path?

00:47:06.179 --> 00:47:09.759
Which path is for toys?

00:47:09.759 --> 00:47:11.039
The less social path, right.

00:47:11.039 --> 00:47:12.719
And so we have a nice kind of,

00:47:12.719 --> 00:47:15.040
a nice design where we can look at familiarity

00:47:15.040 --> 00:47:17.960
for social information and familiar, familiarity 

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:19.889
for non-social information.

00:47:19.889 --> 00:47:20.919
And we had

00:47:20.919 --> 00:47:22.339
a, a reason to,

00:47:22.339 --> 00:47:25.560
to have certain predictions based on typical development. We thought

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:27.020
that in typical development

00:47:27.020 --> 00:47:29.349
we would see different, differential activity

00:47:29.349 --> 00:47:35.029
to familiar versus unfamiliar faces and objects because that's what we'd seen before.

00:47:35.029 --> 00:47:36.589
When we looked at people with autism

00:47:36.589 --> 00:47:39.119
we saw something very different, so these are very young kids.

00:47:39.119 --> 00:47:40.400
These are three-year-olds. 

00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:42.449
So I was there when we collected the data.

00:47:42.449 --> 00:47:46.140
It's, I I cast a rosy picture about the applicability

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:50.860
of ERP for studying a range of developmental
functional levels and a range of

00:47:50.860 --> 00:47:51.449
ages in autism,

00:47:51.449 --> 00:47:53.099
but it's still really an adventure

00:47:53.099 --> 00:47:58.029
to try to do an ERP experiment with a three-year-old
person with autism who doesn't have any language.

00:47:58.029 --> 00:47:59.459
It

00:47:59.459 --> 00:48:03.489
involves lots of energy, lots of toys, and lots of m&amp;ms.

00:48:03.489 --> 00:48:04.359
And so

00:48:04.359 --> 00:48:05.750
what we saw is

00:48:05.750 --> 00:48:07.579
if we look at

00:48:07.579 --> 00:48:10.019
objects and these are both wave forms

00:48:10.019 --> 00:48:12.689
for people with, for the young children with autism,

00:48:12.689 --> 00:48:15.010
we saw this component, the P400,

00:48:15.010 --> 00:48:16.029
it's called,

00:48:16.029 --> 00:48:18.569
and it's associated with recognition.

00:48:18.569 --> 00:48:19.849
So we saw

00:48:19.849 --> 00:48:21.240
an enhancement

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:23.619
to novel objects,

00:48:23.619 --> 00:48:24.999
and, relative

00:48:24.999 --> 00:48:26.619
to familiar objects.

00:48:26.619 --> 00:48:28.339
Now these are faces.

00:48:28.339 --> 00:48:31.019
So we the same, we see the same kind of peak,

00:48:31.019 --> 00:48:38.019
but what's different between the faces and the objects? Right. And so

00:48:50.169 --> 00:48:53.639
we saw that when we look at the non-social information, they look just like

00:48:53.639 --> 00:48:55.759
their typical counterparts.

00:48:55.759 --> 00:49:00.199
For the faces they didn't. They weren't showing differential brain activity.

00:49:00.199 --> 00:49:02.660
And so, when this study came out one of the concerns

00:49:02.660 --> 00:49:05.709
was that we were saying that kids with autism don't
recognize their mother,

00:49:05.709 --> 00:49:07.190
which isn't the case at all.

00:49:07.190 --> 00:49:09.289
I was there. I was running these kids. They,

00:49:09.289 --> 00:49:14.249
they knew their mothers and they often ran to
them

00:49:14.249 --> 00:49:16.639
frantically when we tried to do the ERPs with them.

00:49:16.639 --> 00:49:18.929
They certainly recognize their mothers,

00:49:18.929 --> 00:49:21.409
but it's telling us they're using different mechanisms to do that.

00:49:21.409 --> 00:49:25.839
And that's one of the things I think it's really important
for us to learn about autism.

00:49:25.839 --> 00:49:27.510
What are the compensatory mechanisms?

00:49:27.510 --> 00:49:30.690
So in another aspect of social information processing that we've used ERP to study

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:33.060
in very young children with autism, and these are the same 

00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:34.390
group of three-year-olds,

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:36.509
is emotion recognition.

00:49:36.509 --> 00:49:37.769
And so for this study

00:49:37.769 --> 00:49:39.989
we contrasted their brain responses

00:49:39.989 --> 00:49:41.299
to neutral

00:49:41.299 --> 00:49:42.859
versus fearful faces.

00:49:42.859 --> 00:49:45.609
And fear is a really useful emotion

00:49:45.609 --> 00:49:48.899
to use because fear is closely linked to
the function of the amygdala,

00:49:48.899 --> 00:49:51.920
which is, which is one of the social brain regions

00:49:51.920 --> 00:49:53.410
involved in autism.

00:49:53.410 --> 00:49:55.449
And how do you get a, what a,

00:49:55.449 --> 00:49:56.729
a eight-month-old child

00:49:56.729 --> 00:49:58.139
to show fear.

00:49:58.139 --> 00:50:05.139
You show her her older sister coming at her with one more sticker that she's about to put on her face.

00:50:05.140 --> 00:50:09.900
So we show these three-year-olds neutral and fearful faces and to be clear, we did not show them neutral and fearful faces

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:11.839
of my daughters. These were

00:50:11.839 --> 00:50:15.039
from a standardized series of faces,

00:50:15.039 --> 00:50:18.650
developed the Ackman's series, they're called, but we can't show them

00:50:18.650 --> 00:50:21.150
for the purposes of this lecture.

00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:21.900
And again,

00:50:21.900 --> 00:50:24.430
so this is a different ERP component.

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:27.180
This is, against, it's not a face-specific component, 

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:31.259
it's just kind of associated with salience. It's called the negative slow waves. 

00:50:31.259 --> 00:50:33.879
And in the typical kids

00:50:33.879 --> 00:50:39.279
we saw a big difference between the fearful face
and the neutral face.

00:50:39.279 --> 00:50:45.129
But what did we see in the kids with autism? 

00:50:45.129 --> 00:50:46.309
Difference or no difference?

00:50:46.309 --> 00:50:47.549
No difference.

00:50:47.549 --> 00:50:49.269
And so

00:50:49.269 --> 00:50:52.339
one of the nice things about this study is
that

00:50:52.339 --> 00:50:53.739
ERPs

00:50:53.739 --> 00:50:56.450
are kind of abstract. Right, we do them in

00:50:56.450 --> 00:51:01.799
a, the lab. We can make guesses about how looking at pictures of emotionally faces

00:51:01.799 --> 00:51:02.750
versus neutral faces

00:51:02.750 --> 00:51:05.189
maps onto things that happen in real life,

00:51:05.189 --> 00:51:06.640
but it's kind of a stretch.

00:51:06.640 --> 00:51:12.079
So what we did in this study is we also did this kind of response-to-distress task,

00:51:12.079 --> 00:51:14.630
where graduate students like myself

00:51:14.630 --> 00:51:17.109
played with the kids. We sat at a table. It was videotaped.

00:51:17.109 --> 00:51:19.949
And we had this hammering toy 

00:51:19.949 --> 00:51:20.799
and so

00:51:20.799 --> 00:51:22.729
we would play with this hammering toy

00:51:22.729 --> 00:51:24.219
and we would pretend to

00:51:24.219 --> 00:51:25.660
hit our finger with the hammer

00:51:25.660 --> 00:51:27.849
and then we would pretend to cry

00:51:27.849 --> 00:51:28.990
and we would just

00:51:28.990 --> 00:51:30.489
cry from like a minute.

00:51:30.489 --> 00:51:33.960
And the video, and afterwards we'd look at the videotapes and determine what the kid did.

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:36.089
Did the kid just think "Oh great,

00:51:36.089 --> 00:51:38.610
you know, this guy stopped playing with the hammer. Now it's my turn"?

00:51:38.610 --> 00:51:39.509
Or would the kid

00:51:39.509 --> 00:51:42.839
look said? Would the kid pay attention to me at all?

00:51:42.839 --> 00:51:46.399
And this is one of the times I regret that we can't show the picture because we have this,

00:51:46.399 --> 00:51:48.410
a great picture from that study,

00:51:48.410 --> 00:51:50.010
where I'm sitting there crying

00:51:50.010 --> 00:51:52.929
and a little three-year-old boy with autism is hammering
away,

00:51:52.929 --> 00:51:55.689
but his mother is looking at me, but it's not a

00:51:55.689 --> 00:51:57.549
pathetic look. It's a looking like

00:51:57.549 --> 00:52:01.569
what is wrong with you.

00:52:01.569 --> 00:52:03.580
So, and what we found was actually,

00:52:03.580 --> 00:52:09.350
so, if we, if we wanted to make a jump about how sensitivity, neuro-sensitivity to expressions,

00:52:09.669 --> 00:52:11.770
to emotional expressions, would map on 

00:52:11.770 --> 00:52:15.579
to behavior, we might think of these things being connected. And that's exactly what we saw.

00:52:15.579 --> 00:52:16.899
So a different component,

00:52:16.899 --> 00:52:18.299
the N300,

00:52:18.299 --> 00:52:19.460
it's latency,

00:52:19.460 --> 00:52:21.130
how long it took happen

00:52:21.130 --> 00:52:23.319
was associated with the amount of time 

00:52:23.319 --> 00:52:25.079
these children spent attending

00:52:25.079 --> 00:52:29.149
to feigned distress.

00:52:29.149 --> 00:52:33.679
So looking now at some different work. This was, again, work done at the University of Washington

00:52:33.679 --> 00:52:37.839
and this is work done in adolescents and adults with autism. 

00:52:37.839 --> 00:52:43.109
We took that study done by Shlomo Bentin, the classic 1996 one, and

00:52:43.109 --> 00:52:44.909
looked at the N170

00:52:44.909 --> 00:52:47.369
and people with autism. We should them 

00:52:47.369 --> 00:52:52.439
upright and inverted faces .

00:52:52.439 --> 00:52:53.730
So I describe the N170, but I hadn't 

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:55.040
shown you one. So this is an example of an N170.

00:52:55.040 --> 00:52:57.069
This is from fourteen typical,

00:52:57.069 --> 00:52:59.439
typical adolescents and adults.

00:52:59.439 --> 00:53:01.449
And what we see is that negative

00:53:01.449 --> 00:53:02.819
dip in  electrical activity

00:53:02.819 --> 00:53:07.009
that happens around a hundred seventeen milliseconds, and this is associated with faces. 

00:53:07.009 --> 00:53:08.449
And when we showed

00:53:08.449 --> 00:53:10.759
these same faces to the people with autism,

00:53:10.759 --> 00:53:12.809
we saw a different kind of response.

00:53:12.809 --> 00:53:15.410
The, it did, it wasn't like it didn't have

00:53:15.410 --> 00:53:18.029
an N170, but

00:53:18.029 --> 00:53:21.149
it was different from the typical counterparts. And I
should make clear that these people were

00:53:21.149 --> 00:53:23.309
just as smart as typical counterparts.

00:53:23.309 --> 00:53:27.809
They perform similarly on many non-social
behavioral measures.

00:53:27.809 --> 00:53:30.449
But they had a very different response and they're response

00:53:30.449 --> 00:53:33.449
was actually something that we could only pick up with ERP.

00:53:33.449 --> 00:53:35.659
There's a, they're response was to be slower.

00:53:35.659 --> 00:53:37.359
So while most people have

00:53:37.359 --> 00:53:40.820
N170s around n, around 170, 180 milliseconds,

00:53:40.820 --> 00:53:45.029
the people with autism were much slower.

00:53:45.029 --> 00:53:46.170
Then we also looked at the 

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:49.550
inversion effect. So if we show them the faces upside down

00:53:49.550 --> 00:53:51.529
would we see a different response?

00:53:51.529 --> 00:53:53.969
And so this is the, what an inversion effect

00:53:53.969 --> 00:53:55.209
looks like

00:53:55.209 --> 00:53:56.409
in typical people.

00:53:56.409 --> 00:53:57.130
So you see a big,

00:53:57.130 --> 00:53:59.889
the red light is upside down faces,

00:53:59.889 --> 00:54:02.960
the blue line is the upright faces.

00:54:02.960 --> 00:54:05.449
And so what you see is a bigger,

00:54:05.449 --> 00:54:08.660
slower response to upside down faces

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:09.060
and,

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:13.480
in the kids with autism, the adolescents and adults with autism rather, we say some slightly different

00:54:13.480 --> 00:54:15.119
response. So

00:54:15.119 --> 00:54:17.100
we did see maybe a little difference in amplitude,

00:54:17.100 --> 00:54:20.789
but what was really salient was, again, the timing.

00:54:20.789 --> 00:54:22.449
So, whereas typical people

00:54:22.449 --> 00:54:24.329
slowed down

00:54:24.329 --> 00:54:25.909
when they saw an upside down face,

00:54:25.909 --> 00:54:29.289
people with autism there wasn't any difference. The,
in terms of the timing,

00:54:29.289 --> 00:54:31.910
they're upright were equivalent

00:54:31.910 --> 00:54:36.280
to their inverted faces. And that's shown by the, the slope of these purple lines there,

00:54:36.280 --> 00:54:37.599
those, those purple lines are

00:54:37.599 --> 00:54:40.069
the inversion effect.

00:54:40.069 --> 00:54:44.999
So what we saw in these adolescents and adults were slower processing speed

00:54:44.999 --> 00:54:48.759
and insensitivity to inversion and,

00:54:48.759 --> 00:54:50.579
excuse me.

00:54:50.579 --> 00:54:53.509
Again we wanted to see if we could tie this to something,

00:54:53.509 --> 00:54:55.220
to some kind of behavioral measure

00:54:55.220 --> 00:54:58.899
that we could be sure was more ecologically
valid, or we could be

00:54:58.899 --> 00:54:59.539
optimistic

00:54:59.539 --> 00:55:02.139
was, was ecologically valid.

00:55:02.139 --> 00:55:04.189
And what we, so we administered 

00:55:04.189 --> 00:55:08.279
a standardized test of face recognition. It's part of a memory scale called 

00:55:08.279 --> 00:55:09.009
the Wexler Memory Scale 

00:55:09.009 --> 00:55:12.150
and we looked at how good people were at recognizing faces.

00:55:12.150 --> 00:55:16.450
And so again, despite being comparably intelligent,
people with autism

00:55:16.450 --> 00:55:18.079
made twice as many mistakes

00:55:18.079 --> 00:55:21.249
on a face-recognition task.

00:55:21.249 --> 00:55:22.909
And, moreover, these mistakes

00:55:22.909 --> 00:55:25.439
correlated with their processing speed.

00:55:25.439 --> 00:55:27.239
So how face a person 

00:55:27.239 --> 00:55:28.969
showed this spike to a face 

00:55:28.969 --> 00:55:31.020
was actually associated with how good they were

00:55:31.020 --> 00:55:33.209
at face recognition. 

00:55:33.209 --> 00:55:34.740
So this was actually the first study

00:55:34.740 --> 00:55:36.829
to look at the N170 in autism,

00:55:36.829 --> 00:55:39.989
but since then there have been many studies
and

00:55:39.989 --> 00:55:42.480
what has emerged is that we see that

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:45.429
across a number of studies there's delayed latency
for faces.

00:55:45.429 --> 00:55:48.120
This has been found in children as young

00:55:48.120 --> 00:55:49.759
as three years of age.

00:55:49.759 --> 00:55:51.989
What's very interesting

00:55:51.989 --> 00:55:56.289
is that the same effect has been found in parents of children with autism

00:55:56.289 --> 00:55:58.379
and in a very recent study

00:55:58.379 --> 00:56:00.649
by my colleague Joe McClearly

00:56:00.649 --> 00:56:02.209
in the, in the UK.

00:56:02.209 --> 00:56:03.979
He spent of it in ten-month-olds,

00:56:03.979 --> 00:56:07.069
who are at risk for autism, but haven't been diagnosed.

00:56:07.069 --> 00:56:09.029
We've replicated in other studies

00:56:09.029 --> 00:56:10.619
the intensity to inversion 

00:56:10.619 --> 00:56:11.689
and there have been other

00:56:11.689 --> 00:56:15.340
forms of atypicality. Some studies have found decreased amplitude.

00:56:15.340 --> 00:56:18.199
Some have found abnormal lateralization or

00:56:18.199 --> 00:56:22.559
patterns of activity at the scalp that are more characteristic
of younger children.

00:56:22.559 --> 00:56:25.229
And again as I said before,

00:56:25.229 --> 00:56:31.149
not all of these results are replicated across
all studies. It's really a heterogeneous picture.

00:56:31.149 --> 00:56:35.640
And I'll talk about some manipulations that we're doing now, some experimental manipulations

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:36.579
to understand more

00:56:36.579 --> 00:56:37.739
about why

00:56:37.739 --> 00:56:44.739
we see some effects in some kids and different effects in other kids. It seems like part of the picture you're, you're suggesting that kids process faces like objects. But it seems like in one of the presentations you were showing they don't process faces like objects. They, they recognize objects better than they recognize faces. So, aren't those two things kind of contradictory?

00:57:02.809 --> 00:57:03.829
Well, let me,

00:57:03.829 --> 00:57:06.030
let me go on to the next part as see if, 

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:08.300
I don't think that faces are

00:57:08.300 --> 00:57:09.540
processed like objects.

00:57:09.540 --> 00:57:10.959
I think that

00:57:10.959 --> 00:57:14.550
faces may not be processed in a special way by people with autism.

00:57:14.550 --> 00:57:15.940
But it's actually turns out

00:57:15.940 --> 00:57:17.990
that objects can also be processed

00:57:17.990 --> 00:57:20.109
in a very special way,

00:57:20.109 --> 00:57:21.989
which I'll talk about right now.

00:57:21.989 --> 00:57:26.019
So what do we make of this? Do we think
that people with autism are just born with

00:57:26.019 --> 00:57:30.619
a hole in their brain in their face-processing regions? Do we think that they're

00:57:30.619 --> 00:57:31.929
unable to do

00:57:31.929 --> 00:57:33.299
this kind

00:57:33.299 --> 00:57:34.299
of

00:57:34.299 --> 00:57:36.419
information processing in their brain? No.

00:57:36.419 --> 00:57:38.919
We kind of explain in a developmental way.

00:57:38.919 --> 00:57:39.929
So we know that

00:57:39.929 --> 00:57:41.289
faces

00:57:41.289 --> 00:57:43.010
are processed in special ways.

00:57:43.010 --> 00:57:44.379
We know

00:57:44.379 --> 00:57:46.149
that the,

00:57:46.149 --> 00:57:48.989
the specialization associated with face processing

00:57:48.989 --> 00:57:51.019
happens over time.

00:57:51.019 --> 00:57:52.609
So what if

00:57:52.609 --> 00:57:54.710
people with autism come into the world

00:57:54.710 --> 00:57:57.079
less drawn to other people

00:57:57.079 --> 00:58:02.049
and therefore they fail to, to pay attention
to people and they fail to become face experts.

00:58:02.049 --> 00:58:06.359
So if we think about our path towards social expertise culminating in face expertise,

00:58:06.359 --> 00:58:08.859
we could think about people with autism failing to
chart

00:58:08.859 --> 00:58:09.670
that course.

00:58:09.670 --> 00:58:13.329
And so we published this and we called it the social
motivation hypothesis.

00:58:13.329 --> 00:58:15.660
And the idea is that the problem in autism

00:58:15.660 --> 00:58:18.379
is really decreased social motivation,

00:58:18.379 --> 00:58:20.709
which leads to inattention to faces,

00:58:20.709 --> 00:58:23.959
which leads to a failure develop expertise

00:58:23.959 --> 00:58:25.309
and then that's reflected in the,

00:58:25.309 --> 00:58:27.050
the atypical

00:58:27.050 --> 00:58:30.579
specialization we see both in terms of brain and behavior.

00:58:30.579 --> 00:58:33.849
But there's an important presumption here, and this is something
that been very interesting

00:58:33.849 --> 00:58:37.769
to me that I've studied over the past couple of years here at Yale

00:58:37.769 --> 00:58:39.189
is, is this piece. 

00:58:39.189 --> 00:58:43.649
I started off today talking about how we have different explanations for autism and it,

00:58:43.649 --> 00:58:47.419
it might be a social problem, but social information could all just be a red herring.

00:58:47.419 --> 00:58:50.590
Maybe it's a problem with complex information processing.

00:58:50.590 --> 00:58:52.809
And we know

00:58:52.809 --> 00:58:54.370
that the development of expertise

00:58:54.370 --> 00:58:55.400
is something

00:58:55.400 --> 00:58:58.630
that requires complex information processing.

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:00.150
So what if people with autism

00:59:00.150 --> 00:59:01.450
just have a problem

00:59:01.450 --> 00:59:03.070
developing perceptual expertise?

00:59:03.070 --> 00:59:04.859
So how can we look at that and

00:59:04.859 --> 00:59:07.479
what is perceptual expertise?

00:59:07.479 --> 00:59:09.589
So when we talk about perceptual expertise 

00:59:09.589 --> 00:59:13.089
it's the idea that as we become really good 

00:59:13.089 --> 00:59:15.350
we get a lot of experience

00:59:15.350 --> 00:59:17.430
distinguishing among things that are very
similar

00:59:17.430 --> 00:59:18.640
like faces

00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:21.669
or, if you are a monkey expert, like monkeys

00:59:21.669 --> 00:59:23.280
we start to treat them different.

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:24.720
So we start to treat

00:59:24.720 --> 00:59:26.979
the faces,

00:59:26.979 --> 00:59:32.879
we start to treat those objects or those non-face things the way we treat faces. We starts to see signs of

00:59:32.879 --> 00:59:33.989
holistic processing.

00:59:33.989 --> 00:59:35.700
We start to see inversion effects.

00:59:35.700 --> 00:59:39.379
We start to see activity in the fusiform gyrus, and most relevant to us,

00:59:39.379 --> 00:59:43.279
we start to see enhanced N170 amplitude.

00:59:43.279 --> 00:59:44.409
One thing

00:59:44.409 --> 00:59:46.769
that hasn't been studied yet what, well, isn't,

00:59:46.769 --> 00:59:48.320
isn't will understand it yet

00:59:48.320 --> 00:59:51.999
is to what piece your affective experience plays in.

00:59:51.999 --> 00:59:54.449
So I have there a, an example of a car

00:59:54.449 --> 00:59:56.089
and a bird and those are both

00:59:56.089 --> 00:59:59.279
things that have been studied in neuroscience

00:59:59.279 --> 00:59:59.930
research

00:59:59.930 --> 01:00:04.729
to show expertise effects. When a person gets really good at individuating among birds or

01:00:04.729 --> 01:00:05.910
different kinds of cars,

01:00:05.910 --> 01:00:07.489
they get a bigger N170

01:00:07.489 --> 01:00:12.039
than people who don't care about them.

01:00:12.039 --> 01:00:14.849
But what hasn't, but what we don't know is,

01:00:14.849 --> 01:00:18.559
it's rare that a person develops some kind of
expertise

01:00:18.559 --> 01:00:20.690
without some kind of personal interest.

01:00:20.690 --> 01:00:24.050
So car experts aren't just car experts in the way.

01:00:24.050 --> 01:00:25.990
You know, like if you think about a baggage handler,

01:00:25.990 --> 01:00:27.520
they see lots of bags,

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:32.079
but they probably feel differently about bags than car experts feel about cars, right.

01:00:32.079 --> 01:00:34.170
And so that's something that isn't studied.

01:00:34.170 --> 01:00:38.339
And I think it's relevant to studying perceptual expertise and autism cause the two ways 

01:00:38.339 --> 01:00:41.059
that people have gone about it so far

01:00:41.059 --> 01:00:45.069
are to either train people with autism to be expert in
something

01:00:45.069 --> 01:00:47.699
or to look at something they're expert in already.

01:00:47.699 --> 01:00:50.220
So the training studies teach a person

01:00:50.220 --> 01:00:52.089
to become experts in a particular area.

01:00:52.089 --> 01:00:56.740
What's nice about this is it makes for a very
clean elegant experience, experiment

01:00:56.740 --> 01:00:58.469
because you have a group of people then

01:00:58.469 --> 01:01:00.599
who have

01:01:00.599 --> 01:01:03.540
all comparable degrees of expertise.

01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:05.719
You've controlled their access to it, you know.

01:01:05.719 --> 01:01:10.259
But what the problem's with it is it seems not really the way we develop expertise in real life.

01:01:10.259 --> 01:01:12.040
So there's not intrinsic interest

01:01:12.040 --> 01:01:14.049
and there's not much affective

01:01:14.049 --> 01:01:15.269
involvement we would assume.

01:01:15.269 --> 01:01:19.070
Now naturalistic studies seem to solve those problems. People become experts in things 

01:01:19.070 --> 01:01:20.259
because they're interested in them.

01:01:20.259 --> 01:01:22.620
And many people with autism

01:01:22.620 --> 01:01:25.319
really become experts in things.

01:01:25.319 --> 01:01:26.939
The problem is, is that

01:01:26.939 --> 01:01:29.139
it's hard to find groups of children

01:01:29.139 --> 01:01:31.719
who are all expert in the same thing,

01:01:31.719 --> 01:01:35.349
so that we can do a clean experiment.

01:01:35.349 --> 01:01:36.859
So, this is a problem that

01:01:36.859 --> 01:01:39.489
we had kind of thought about quite a bit.

01:01:39.489 --> 01:01:42.220
One of the nice things about being a clinician

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:46.109
is it sometimes you don't have to have good
ideas, good ideas

01:01:46.109 --> 01:01:47.059
kind of walk into your clique.

01:01:47.059 --> 01:01:50.469
And so one day I was working with a kid, a four-year-old boy,

01:01:50.469 --> 01:01:53.809
who was very bright. We had followed him since he was two.

01:01:53.809 --> 01:01:54.689
I'm talking about this,

01:01:54.689 --> 01:01:56.449
this is my favorite video to show,

01:01:56.449 --> 01:01:57.709
but, again, we can't show it,

01:01:57.709 --> 01:02:00.939
so we'll do a little re-enactment.

01:02:00.939 --> 01:02:03.030
But we were doing a play session

01:02:03.030 --> 01:02:03.509
and

01:02:03.509 --> 01:02:04.200
he would,

01:02:04.200 --> 01:02:09.689
he would say things different, he, he would see things very differently than I would.

01:02:09.689 --> 01:02:11.969
So, you are unfortunate to be sitting right up front.

01:02:11.969 --> 01:02:18.929
So you'll, you can be, you're going to be the boy. So what's
in this bag? W.

01:02:18.929 --> 01:02:22.309
I, I was a, I was afraid that was going to happen.

01:02:22.309 --> 01:02:24.769
The, these are phone blocks.

01:02:24.769 --> 01:02:27.769
We'll try a different one.

01:02:27.769 --> 01:02:30.239
No, you, I got it wrong.

01:02:30.239 --> 01:02:31.079
Okay.

01:02:31.079 --> 01:02:33.949
What, what do you see there?

01:02:33.949 --> 01:02:34.939
A boy snorkeling.

01:02:34.939 --> 01:02:38.519
Can you tell me anything else about it? No. Okay.

01:02:38.519 --> 01:02:45.519
So these are, these are pieces of actually a diagnostic assessment that we use called the ADOS.

01:02:45.749 --> 01:02:47.039
Whenever I would bring out 

01:02:47.039 --> 01:02:48.189
the boy would

01:02:48.189 --> 01:02:50.489
see as a letter.

01:02:50.489 --> 01:02:53.640
So, the boy and one of my students saw

01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:55.170
these as Ws.

01:02:55.170 --> 01:02:56.450
To me they were just blocks.

01:02:56.450 --> 01:02:58.089
When I brought this out

01:02:58.089 --> 01:03:02.699
I said "Oh look! There's a guy snorkeling." And he said "Yeah, the snorkel is a J."

01:03:02.699 --> 01:03:03.549
When I brought this

01:03:03.549 --> 01:03:08.959
out I didn't even realize this was happening. I brought past fast his face really quickly and he said "WPS." I

01:03:08.959 --> 01:03:11.069
said "WPS?" And then I looked oh

01:03:11.069 --> 01:03:14.179
Western Psychological Services, the company
that makes it.

01:03:14.179 --> 01:03:17.220
So this boy was seeing letters

01:03:17.220 --> 01:03:17.789
everywhere.

01:03:17.789 --> 01:03:23.539
And it actually turns out it's a pretty common
area of expertise in autism.

01:03:23.539 --> 01:03:26.080
So many young children with autism

01:03:26.080 --> 01:03:27.869
get the very interested in letters.

01:03:27.869 --> 01:03:32.259
We're not exactly sure why. We think that because
letters are omnipresent

01:03:32.259 --> 01:03:36.369
and they're nice because their always the
same twenty-six

01:03:36.369 --> 01:03:38.499
and they're always in the sequence,

01:03:38.499 --> 01:03:39.420
so they, they kind of fit,

01:03:39.420 --> 01:03:41.669
fit the rules for a person who likes ritual

01:03:41.669 --> 01:03:42.779
and routine.

01:03:42.779 --> 01:03:44.889
We find that's an area of strength for

01:03:44.889 --> 01:03:46.470
people on the spectrum.

01:03:46.470 --> 01:03:47.339
Even

01:03:47.339 --> 01:03:49.730
though problems with language

01:03:49.730 --> 01:03:50.609
is a, a,

01:03:50.609 --> 01:03:52.510
are a really salient part

01:03:52.510 --> 01:03:53.460
of autism,

01:03:53.460 --> 01:03:56.969
we see intact word reading and decoding in most children 

01:03:56.969 --> 01:04:00.669
with autism or at least what would be predicted by their intellectual level.

01:04:00.669 --> 01:04:02.459
And then there's a subgroup of children,

01:04:02.459 --> 01:04:05.190
who have what we call hyper-lexia, who are really

01:04:05.190 --> 01:04:07.990
precocious, self-taught readers.

01:04:07.990 --> 01:04:09.980
And that's a five to fifteen percent prevalence.

01:04:09.980 --> 01:04:13.950
A lot of the really good work in hyper-lexia
has been done right here by Elena Grigorenko

01:04:13.950 --> 01:04:15.649
and Tina Newman

01:04:15.649 --> 01:04:16.449
out of

01:04:16.449 --> 01:04:18.199
Naples. 

01:04:18.199 --> 01:04:19.239
So

01:04:19.239 --> 01:04:21.399
we've got an area

01:04:21.399 --> 01:04:25.159
in which many people with autism develop an expertise, but

01:04:25.159 --> 01:04:28.599
it's only good to us as people who are interested in
the brain,

01:04:28.599 --> 01:04:32.559
if it gives us a way to measure precept, perceptual expertise in terms

01:04:32.559 --> 01:04:33.769
of brain function.

01:04:33.769 --> 01:04:35.679
And it turns out it does.

01:04:35.679 --> 01:04:38.140
So a colleague Alan Wong, who at the time

01:04:38.140 --> 01:04:40.099
was a graduate student with Isabel Gauthier 

01:04:40.099 --> 01:04:43.869
at Vanderbilt and is now at the University of Hong Kong

01:04:43.869 --> 01:04:47.150
looked at how people learn to process letters

01:04:47.150 --> 01:04:49.449
and he did a very nice experiment,

01:04:49.449 --> 01:04:52.269
where he took letters in the Roman alphabet,

01:04:52.269 --> 01:04:53.589
which is what we read,

01:04:53.589 --> 01:04:55.739
you know, what you're seeing here.

01:04:55.739 --> 01:04:57.660
Then he took Chinese letters

01:04:57.660 --> 01:05:00.459
and then he made up an alphabet

01:05:00.459 --> 01:05:04.439
and he showed these letters to people and measured their brain response.

01:05:04.439 --> 01:05:06.329
And what he found

01:05:06.329 --> 01:05:08.859
was that you got an enhanced N170 

01:05:08.859 --> 01:05:10.750
if you could read the letters of an alphabet. It

01:05:10.750 --> 01:05:12.189
didn't matter which alphabet.

01:05:12.189 --> 01:05:15.320
So if you were bilingual reading Chinese in
English

01:05:15.320 --> 01:05:18.820
you get a bigger N170 to both Chinese characters and Roman characters.

01:05:18.820 --> 01:05:21.279
If you were a person who could only read English,

01:05:21.279 --> 01:05:22.440
then you'd get bigger

01:05:22.440 --> 01:05:25.000
N170 to Roman characters, but not to

01:05:25.000 --> 01:05:27.689
Chinese or pseudo-font. They were equivalent.

01:05:27.689 --> 01:05:29.310
So what this let's us do then

01:05:29.310 --> 01:05:30.720
is kind of map out 

01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:35.270
those endpoints. So if we think about our social expertise path ending in faces

01:05:35.270 --> 01:05:36.829
we could take a look experimentally

01:05:36.829 --> 01:05:39.429
at a non-social expertise path ending in letters.

01:05:39.429 --> 01:05:42.679
And so we contrast things that we think we're social

01:05:42.679 --> 01:05:44.689
experts in versus not experts in

01:05:44.689 --> 01:05:45.619
with houses

01:05:45.619 --> 01:05:46.829
and then pseudo-letters.

01:05:46.829 --> 01:05:49.129
And this is some of the recent work that we've
done

01:05:49.129 --> 01:05:51.059
in people with autism.

01:05:51.059 --> 01:05:53.259
And so what we've compared is 

01:05:53.259 --> 01:05:57.039
expert and then non-expert stimuli in the social domain, faces versus houses,

01:05:57.039 --> 01:06:00.869
and in the non-social domain, letters versus social letters versus pseudo letters.

01:06:00.869 --> 01:06:03.760
And then what we do is we take the N170 record

01:06:03.760 --> 01:06:08.390
it from this a two-hundred-and-fifty-six electro-net and we record the N170 from patches on the

01:06:08.390 --> 01:06:08.790
right

01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:11.109
and left hemisphere.

01:06:11.109 --> 01:06:12.429
And so for faces

01:06:12.429 --> 01:06:15.569
we saw the kinds of things that we've seen
before.

01:06:15.569 --> 01:06:20.169
This was a younger group of children than I've studied before, but

01:06:20.169 --> 01:06:22.589
we found in our test of face recognition

01:06:22.589 --> 01:06:24.750
they performed significantly worse

01:06:24.750 --> 01:06:29.049
than their typical counterparts, despite being as, as
generally intelligent as them.

01:06:29.049 --> 01:06:32.829
And we saw in the right hemisphere, the hemisphere that's
most often associated

01:06:32.829 --> 01:06:34.909
with face perception,

01:06:34.909 --> 01:06:36.289
we saw that they had, a, a

01:06:36.289 --> 01:06:38.260
slower latency

01:06:38.260 --> 01:06:39.539
just to faces

01:06:39.539 --> 01:06:42.349
than typical individuals.

01:06:42.349 --> 01:06:44.669
When we look at houses, both groups were equivalent.

01:06:44.669 --> 01:06:46.099
I talked earlier about

01:06:46.099 --> 01:06:48.109
source localization and

01:06:48.109 --> 01:06:51.949
we're starting to dabble in it, although we're
not necessarily zealots.

01:06:51.949 --> 01:06:56.710
But for both groups we saw that the N170 localized to the fusiform gyrus.

01:06:56.710 --> 01:06:58.959
So it's not like there's really

01:06:58.959 --> 01:07:01.539
qualitatively different kinds of things happening
in the brains.

01:07:01.539 --> 01:07:03.449
It's just happening in a different pace.

01:07:03.449 --> 01:07:05.630
But what was really intricate, interesting to us

01:07:05.630 --> 01:07:07.400
was letters. So first we looked at,

01:07:07.400 --> 01:07:09.260
at behavior. We looked at reading

01:07:09.260 --> 01:07:11.289
in two different ways.

01:07:11.289 --> 01:07:13.279
So we can look at reading

01:07:13.279 --> 01:07:15.759
words.

01:07:15.759 --> 01:07:18.119
Reading words can happen in, in, in,

01:07:18.119 --> 01:07:22.549
with different kinds of strategies. We might recognize them based on the shape of the word or we might sound, sound 

01:07:22.549 --> 01:07:23.290
them out.

01:07:23.290 --> 01:07:26.010
But we can force a person to sound them out

01:07:26.010 --> 01:07:27.539
testing their mastery of all the,

01:07:27.539 --> 01:07:29.799
the individual pieces of the word

01:07:29.799 --> 01:07:31.029
if we make up words.

01:07:31.029 --> 01:07:34.679
So if we use words that nobody has ever seen before. The only way you can pronounce it correctly

01:07:34.679 --> 01:07:36.769
is you understand the rules

01:07:36.769 --> 01:07:37.750
of, of

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:38.900
sounding out words. And

01:07:38.900 --> 01:07:41.309
on, on both kinds of measures

01:07:41.309 --> 01:07:45.000
people with autism scored just like their typical
counterparts.

01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:47.030
And both groups scored in the average range.

01:07:47.030 --> 01:07:49.519
So we know because this is a norm test

01:07:49.519 --> 01:07:53.089
that it's not something idiosyncratic about people who live in New Haven.

01:07:53.089 --> 01:07:58.609
They're performing just as well as anybody in their age range would.

01:07:58.609 --> 01:08:03.269
When we look at the N170, the first question really was would we see an enhanced N170 

01:08:03.269 --> 01:08:05.900
to letters even in the typical

01:08:05.900 --> 01:08:08.900
adolescents and children

01:08:08.900 --> 01:08:10.360
adolescents because it hadn't been really looked at

01:08:10.360 --> 01:08:12.710
in a group this young before. And that is what we saw.

01:08:12.710 --> 01:08:14.720
So the pink highlights the N170.

01:08:14.720 --> 01:08:17.820
And you see the purple line is the letters. The green
line is the pseudo-letters.

01:08:17.820 --> 01:08:19.810
And we see a larger amplitude 

01:08:19.810 --> 01:08:20.909
to letters.

01:08:20.909 --> 01:08:23.199
And what we see in the group of autism we did.

01:08:23.199 --> 01:08:29.449
We actually saw a, a bigger difference between
letters and pseudo-letters in the group with autism.

01:08:29.449 --> 01:08:30.929
So

01:08:30.929 --> 01:08:31.799
when we,

01:08:31.799 --> 01:08:34.099
when we compared the groups,

01:08:34.099 --> 01:08:37.099
both groups showed an enhanced amplitude to letters.

01:08:37.099 --> 01:08:40.689
There was no differences between the groups.

01:08:40.689 --> 01:08:45.420
The typical group showed that the expected pattern of left-lateralized  response.

01:08:45.420 --> 01:08:47.279
But

01:08:47.279 --> 01:08:51.649
the group with autism showed bilateral specialization. You can really see it here.

01:08:51.649 --> 01:08:51.999
So this is a,

01:08:51.999 --> 01:08:53.849
a picture, kind of a,

01:08:53.849 --> 01:08:56.060
think of it as a heat map and

01:08:56.060 --> 01:08:58.639
the darker the purple color shows the more negative

01:08:58.639 --> 01:09:00.289
the activity.

01:09:00.289 --> 01:09:01.720
And so what we've found is

01:09:01.720 --> 01:09:06.170
the typical group will see things localized to the left. The autism group actually recruited right

01:09:06.170 --> 01:09:08.259
hemisphere regions, which is kind of interesting

01:09:08.259 --> 01:09:10.120
because those are the regions typically involved

01:09:10.120 --> 01:09:13.729
in face perception.

01:09:13.729 --> 01:09:14.670
So

01:09:14.670 --> 01:09:18.589
we see in this experiment, we see that they have

01:09:18.589 --> 01:09:19.230
impaired face recognition.

01:09:19.230 --> 01:09:21.369
Atypical neuro-responses to faces 

01:09:21.369 --> 01:09:22.759
they show

01:09:22.759 --> 01:09:24.180
intact word reading

01:09:24.180 --> 01:09:26.079
and preserved,

01:09:26.079 --> 01:09:28.129
kind of enhanced

01:09:28.129 --> 01:09:30.109
neuro-response to letters

01:09:30.109 --> 01:09:33.459
with the recruitment of the right hemisphere. So what

01:09:33.459 --> 01:09:34.230
does this tell us about autism?

01:09:34.230 --> 01:09:35.579
Well it makes the case

01:09:35.579 --> 01:09:37.369
that their brains,

01:09:37.369 --> 01:09:39.360
their brains can learn. None of us are born

01:09:39.360 --> 01:09:40.670
learning to read, I'm pretty sure.

01:09:40.670 --> 01:09:43.260
We have Yale students, if anybody could, you guys could.

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:46.099
And I'm pretty sure you couldn't.

01:09:46.099 --> 01:09:48.969
So we know that these are things they've learned over the course of development.

01:09:48.969 --> 01:09:51.409
We know that to learn these things

01:09:51.409 --> 01:09:55.639
is kind of a complicated brain operation.

01:09:55.639 --> 01:09:58.800
It requires your prefrontal cortex to be compute, communicated with cortical regions way in the back.

01:09:58.800 --> 01:10:00.309
So we know that,

01:10:00.309 --> 01:10:02.930
that things like this can work in the brain.

01:10:02.930 --> 01:10:07.080
It tells us we need a more nuanced story.

01:10:07.080 --> 01:10:09.010
So we need to look at connectivity within specific systems

01:10:09.010 --> 01:10:10.980
rather than just generic dysfunction.

01:10:10.980 --> 01:10:13.090
And this also gives us encouragement that the 

01:10:13.090 --> 01:10:15.839
all these different kinds of interventions
that we do

01:10:15.839 --> 01:10:18.360
aiming to improve social information processing 

01:10:18.360 --> 01:10:21.599
by driving people with autism to attend to these
things

01:10:21.599 --> 01:10:23.349
may payoff.

01:10:23.349 --> 01:10:24.949
So now I want to talk about,

01:10:24.949 --> 01:10:26.820
this is a,

01:10:26.820 --> 01:10:28.040
this is all the

01:10:28.040 --> 01:10:30.570
more cutting-edge stuff. So this is stuff that, that

01:10:30.570 --> 01:10:32.999
isn't published, hasn't been talked about before.

01:10:32.999 --> 01:10:37.380
These are some of the things that we're working
on now.

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:38.800
So I've alluded to this 

01:10:38.800 --> 01:10:39.630
earlier, but

01:10:39.630 --> 01:10:40.539
one of the, I,

01:10:40.539 --> 01:10:43.090
one of the things we're very interested in understanding now is

01:10:43.090 --> 01:10:44.189
why,

01:10:44.189 --> 01:10:47.199
what accounts for the heterogeneity in autism,

01:10:47.199 --> 01:10:48.670
especially in terms of faces?

01:10:48.670 --> 01:10:53.330
So why do we see some kids showing delayed responding and other kids not showing 

01:10:53.330 --> 01:10:54.469
delayed responses

01:10:54.469 --> 01:10:56.329
across studies?

01:10:56.329 --> 01:11:00.119
And there's different kinds of ideas that have been
put forward to explain it.

01:11:00.119 --> 01:11:03.169
One involves visual attention and this is pretty
straightforward.

01:11:03.169 --> 01:11:08.329
If people with autism are always looking at
different parts of the face

01:11:08.329 --> 01:11:10.229
than typically developing people,

01:11:10.229 --> 01:11:12.059
we're going to expect

01:11:12.059 --> 01:11:12.980
different

01:11:12.980 --> 01:11:14.349
patterns of brain activity.

01:11:14.349 --> 01:11:16.919
So we could look at how visual attention

01:11:16.919 --> 01:11:17.920
maps onto

01:11:17.920 --> 01:11:19.639
face perception.

01:11:19.639 --> 01:11:21.260
Another piece is social motivation.

01:11:21.260 --> 01:11:22.650
And this was a, a, a

01:11:22.650 --> 01:11:26.210
neat interaction for me. This was a group of parents,

01:11:26.210 --> 01:11:29.449
actually were here at the Child Study Center, and I gave a talk on so much of this.

01:11:29.449 --> 01:11:34.300
I showed our model social motivation hypothesis and a guy came up to me afterwards

01:11:34.300 --> 01:11:36.439
and he said "Jamie,

01:11:36.439 --> 01:11:38.559
what you just said doesn't make any sense.

01:11:38.559 --> 01:11:40.449
My son has autism,

01:11:40.449 --> 01:11:42.510
but he is super socially motivated.

01:11:42.510 --> 01:11:46.750
He has spent his whole life getting in people's
faces trying to interact with them

01:11:46.750 --> 01:11:50.139
and he's a really odd, awkward, and often unsuccessful approach,

01:11:50.139 --> 01:11:51.589
but he's very motivated."

01:11:51.589 --> 01:11:53.230
And it's a very good point, right.

01:11:53.230 --> 01:11:54.800
In 1979,

01:11:54.800 --> 01:11:59.020
Lorna Wing talked about different groups of children who,

01:11:59.020 --> 01:12:00.820
who were active, but odd

01:12:00.820 --> 01:12:03.389
versus passive versus aloof.

01:12:03.389 --> 01:12:05.199
And we really don't have a sense

01:12:05.199 --> 01:12:07.270
of how those kinds of differences

01:12:07.270 --> 01:12:11.419
within the very diverse group of people
that we would call people with autism

01:12:11.419 --> 01:12:13.690
maps onto difference in brain activity.

01:12:13.690 --> 01:12:16.200
And then finally the third potential explanation

01:12:16.200 --> 01:12:18.900
is looking at

01:12:18.900 --> 01:12:21.960
connectivity within these face perception systems

01:12:21.960 --> 01:12:24.060
or dysfunction within individual components cause

01:12:24.060 --> 01:12:25.840
We've really talk today

01:12:25.840 --> 01:12:27.800
about face processing

01:12:27.800 --> 01:12:28.870
as a thing,

01:12:28.870 --> 01:12:30.679
but it's not. It's really the, a,

01:12:30.679 --> 01:12:33.900
the collection of a lot of individual things
that happen in the brain.

01:12:33.900 --> 01:12:36.269
So I'm going to now show you some preliminary data

01:12:36.269 --> 01:12:37.230
that we've,

01:12:37.230 --> 01:12:40.980
that we're using to start to look at these things.

01:12:40.980 --> 01:12:42.609
So in terms of visual attention,

01:12:42.609 --> 01:12:46.949
what we've done, and this is with a, a graduate student who was hear last year,

01:12:46.949 --> 01:12:48.250
who's now getting her PHD

01:12:48.250 --> 01:12:50.320
at the Institute of Psychology in the UK,

01:12:50.320 --> 01:12:52.150
Celeste Chung, 

01:12:52.150 --> 01:12:53.649
and we've,

01:12:53.649 --> 01:12:55.440
well, if you want to know

01:12:55.440 --> 01:12:59.469
how looking at different parts of a face
could affect the neuro-response,

01:12:59.469 --> 01:13:01.939
what kind of experiment could you do?

01:13:01.939 --> 01:13:08.939
Right, what could you do? 

01:13:10.090 --> 01:13:14.750
Sure. You could do that. And that's actually been done. The very first N170 paper showed different 

01:13:14.750 --> 01:13:16.359
chunks of faces, different pieces, but

01:13:16.359 --> 01:13:17.210
the problem is you're,

01:13:17.210 --> 01:13:19.619
you're changing the face. You're just showing the eyes.

01:13:19.619 --> 01:13:22.780
But one thing you can do is force them to look at different parts of the faces, right.

01:13:22.780 --> 01:13:26.380
So you can have a cross-hair on the screen that makes them look at the eyes or

01:13:26.380 --> 01:13:27.690
makes them look at the nose 

01:13:27.690 --> 01:13:28.550
or makes them look at the mouth.

01:13:28.550 --> 01:13:29.720
And this is what we've actually been doing

01:13:29.720 --> 01:13:31.460
and this is in typical adults.

01:13:31.460 --> 01:13:32.700
And the idea is that

01:13:32.700 --> 01:13:37.940
maybe people with autism are showing different brain activity
because they tend to look at the mouths

01:13:37.940 --> 01:13:38.969
rather than the eyes.

01:13:38.969 --> 01:13:42.449
And indeed we expected that when we force people
to look at the eyes,

01:13:42.449 --> 01:13:45.459
we'd get stronger brain activity,

01:13:45.459 --> 01:13:46.660
a stronger N170

01:13:46.660 --> 01:13:50.119
than if we forced them to look at anywhere else, but that's actually not what we're finding.

01:13:50.119 --> 01:13:51.959
We're finding that

01:13:51.959 --> 01:13:55.010
whether you look at the eyes or whether you look at the mouth
you get a bigger

01:13:55.010 --> 01:13:56.120
N170

01:13:56.120 --> 01:13:59.070
than if you look at the center of the face or if there's no cross-hair at all.

01:13:59.070 --> 01:14:00.150
So, it's almost like

01:14:00.150 --> 01:14:02.800
looking at information, which parts of the
face

01:14:02.800 --> 01:14:04.520
elicit a bigger N170

01:14:04.520 --> 01:14:05.440
than just eyes.

01:14:05.440 --> 01:14:06.710
In isolation

01:14:06.710 --> 01:14:10.179
the eyes actually elicit a bigger N170.

01:14:10.179 --> 01:14:12.130
So we're now starting to do this

01:14:12.130 --> 01:14:14.699
paradigm with people with autism. The question will be

01:14:14.699 --> 01:14:17.179
when we compare people with autism

01:14:17.179 --> 01:14:19.450
and typically developing peers,

01:14:19.450 --> 01:14:21.340
controlling for where they're looking,

01:14:21.340 --> 01:14:22.869
are we still going to find

01:14:22.869 --> 01:14:26.300
these same kinds of brain differences or, or is it really explained away 

01:14:26.300 --> 01:14:27.249
by, by

01:14:27.249 --> 01:14:30.709
where they look. And hopefully next week Kevin Pelphrey will talk to you about some

01:14:30.709 --> 01:14:32.999
of the really neat FMRI work that

01:14:32.999 --> 01:14:35.559
he's done showing that when you control,

01:14:35.559 --> 01:14:39.459
when you manipulate a person's scan path in the magnet, you can make typical

01:14:39.459 --> 01:14:42.380
people look like they have the brain activity of a person with autism

01:14:42.380 --> 01:14:43.840
and vice versa. 

01:14:43.840 --> 01:14:47.599
So the other piece is social motivation.

01:14:47.599 --> 01:14:49.440
So we're, we haven't yet

01:14:49.440 --> 01:14:52.510
started to tease apart groups of people with autism

01:14:52.510 --> 01:14:53.510
with social, who

01:14:53.510 --> 01:14:57.349
are motivated for social interaction, who are, who are more aloof,

01:14:57.349 --> 01:15:00.710
but we're actually looking at it in the typical population because 

01:15:00.710 --> 01:15:05.760
if you think about it, we all vary, right. There are people in this room who are going to spend

01:15:05.760 --> 01:15:06.650
Friday night in, you know,

01:15:06.650 --> 01:15:10.500
in some party, talking to lots of people and there's going to be people in this room who are going to spend Friday night

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:14.480
in the library. I'm assuming you all are going to be Friday night in the library, reading

01:15:14.480 --> 01:15:16.139
about autism, but I know that's not true.

01:15:16.139 --> 01:15:19.949
Right, and so you might say that the people who are at the party are

01:15:19.949 --> 01:15:22.820
extroverts and the people who are at the library are

01:15:22.820 --> 01:15:23.409
introverts.

01:15:23.409 --> 01:15:28.539
And so actually, what we've done is we've brought, we've screened people to be really

01:15:28.539 --> 01:15:30.480
extreme introverts or extreme

01:15:30.480 --> 01:15:33.089
extroverts and we've brought them in for ERPs.

01:15:33.089 --> 01:15:35.180
And we've really been surprised by what we've found,

01:15:35.180 --> 01:15:40.160
really striking differences in brain activity. And these are all people, these aren't people with autism.

01:15:40.160 --> 01:15:42.409
These are people out and about.

01:15:42.409 --> 01:15:43.830
But if you look in blue

01:15:43.830 --> 01:15:49.760
are extroverts and if you look at red are introverts. And again, this is work done by Celeste Chung.

01:15:49.760 --> 01:15:51.979
You see that

01:15:51.979 --> 01:15:54.530
the extroverts have bigger N170s

01:15:54.530 --> 01:15:56.250
and really what's also salient

01:15:56.250 --> 01:15:57.230
is

01:15:57.230 --> 01:15:59.500
extroverts show an inversion

01:15:59.500 --> 01:16:01.019
effects and introverts don't.

01:16:01.019 --> 01:16:04.950
So this is very much in line with the idea that social

01:16:04.950 --> 01:16:06.579
motivation shapes

01:16:06.579 --> 01:16:09.699
our brain activity. We can think about it in developmental way or we could also

01:16:09.699 --> 01:16:13.170
think about it in terms of a salience way. Maybe social information is just more salient in the moment

01:16:13.170 --> 01:16:14.280
for extroverts.

01:16:14.280 --> 01:16:16.959
And then now is a,

01:16:16.959 --> 01:16:20.489
is a, this is a big slide, so I'll, I'll get you ready for it.

01:16:20.489 --> 01:16:22.320
The idea that

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:27.579
face perception, I've talked about the N170, but the N170 represents one

01:16:27.579 --> 01:16:29.460
stage in a series of stages

01:16:29.460 --> 01:16:32.090
of face, of, of the face processing

01:16:32.090 --> 01:16:32.450
system.

01:16:32.450 --> 01:16:35.320
And so what we can do because of the temporal

01:16:35.320 --> 01:16:35.760
acuity

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:38.010
of ERP, we can tease it apart.

01:16:38.010 --> 01:16:40.940
So this is kind of a mock-up

01:16:40.940 --> 01:16:42.340
of a theoretical,

01:16:42.340 --> 01:16:46.520
a, a, a possible explanation of the way face processing 

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:49.289
works in the brain. So if we think about a component called the P100,

01:16:49.289 --> 01:16:51.270
showing us basic visual processing,

01:16:51.270 --> 01:16:54.860
the N170 showing us structural encoding

01:16:54.860 --> 01:17:00.109
and things like the N250 showing us higher-order face processing like affect recognition or identity

01:17:00.109 --> 01:17:03.360
recognition, we can think about how those things tie in

01:17:03.360 --> 01:17:05.309
to the mirror neuron system

01:17:05.309 --> 01:17:10.360
and then we think about how those things tie into related behaviors like imitation or face perception

01:17:10.360 --> 01:17:13.199
and then how those things influence

01:17:13.199 --> 01:17:16.329
vie, how it influences social interaction. And we have ways

01:17:16.329 --> 01:17:17.139
of measuring social interaction

01:17:17.139 --> 01:17:19.150
in real life. And so we've actually

01:17:19.150 --> 01:17:20.360
just submitted this

01:17:20.360 --> 01:17:22.889
as a grant actually today.

01:17:22.889 --> 01:17:23.699
A grant

01:17:23.699 --> 01:17:26.560
went in to do a study with College of

01:17:26.560 --> 01:17:29.869
the University of Washington to look at just this, to

01:17:29.869 --> 01:17:32.810
use ERP to break face processing into

01:17:32.810 --> 01:17:33.949
individual 

01:17:33.949 --> 01:17:36.139
components and then to see, well, if we are finding that

01:17:36.139 --> 01:17:38.070
some people aren't,

01:17:38.070 --> 01:17:40.239
some people have delays

01:17:40.239 --> 01:17:42.900
in the N170 and other people don't,

01:17:42.900 --> 01:17:44.360
maybe if we look at the

01:17:44.360 --> 01:17:48.300
broader array of, of neuro-functions associated

01:17:48.300 --> 01:17:49.349
with face perception 

01:17:49.349 --> 01:17:53.540
and beyond, we're going to start to see different profiles. So maybe there's

01:17:53.540 --> 01:17:56.659
slow N170ers who compensate with fast N250s

01:17:56.659 --> 01:17:57.489
and

01:17:57.489 --> 01:18:01.079
actually look okay here. Or maybe they're slow N170 people 

01:18:01.079 --> 01:18:02.790
who have even slower

01:18:02.790 --> 01:18:03.679
N250s and

01:18:03.679 --> 01:18:04.810
have really big problems here.

01:18:04.810 --> 01:18:06.020
But with,

01:18:06.020 --> 01:18:08.580
with statistical approaches like structural

01:18:08.580 --> 01:18:09.989
equation modeling 

01:18:09.989 --> 01:18:11.070
we can actually test the directionality

01:18:11.070 --> 01:18:12.230
of these arrows

01:18:12.230 --> 01:18:13.880
and look at in an empirical way

01:18:13.880 --> 01:18:15.120
which models

01:18:15.120 --> 01:18:17.860
best fit different groups of people. So we can
compare

01:18:17.860 --> 01:18:19.600
for sure people with autism

01:18:19.600 --> 01:18:21.969
and typical people, but even more interestingly,

01:18:21.969 --> 01:18:24.210
we can then try to tease apart

01:18:24.210 --> 01:18:28.310
different groups of people within the category
of autism.

01:18:28.310 --> 01:18:29.890
And this, I should say,

01:18:29.890 --> 01:18:32.140
this is developed with my colleague Ralph Bernier

01:18:32.140 --> 01:18:34.400
at the University of Washington.

01:18:34.400 --> 01:18:36.570
And then

01:18:36.570 --> 01:18:37.880
the next step

01:18:37.880 --> 01:18:41.440
is, this is work that we're not doing yet, but it is
imminent,

01:18:41.440 --> 01:18:43.679
we're going to take advantage of

01:18:43.679 --> 01:18:45.610
the applicability of ERP

01:18:45.610 --> 01:18:49.250
to babies and we are going to start doing ERPs on little babies

01:18:49.250 --> 01:18:52.400
who are at risk for autism to try to track

01:18:52.400 --> 01:18:54.760
the development of social behavior in a 

01:18:54.760 --> 01:18:57.720
prospective way. And what we're also going to try to do, we're already

01:18:57.720 --> 01:19:00.380
doing in adults, is try to get around that problem

01:19:00.380 --> 01:19:03.510
of what people are looking at by recording ERP and I-tracking at the same time.

01:19:03.510 --> 01:19:06.849
So with Dr. Clinton and Warren Jones,

01:19:06.849 --> 01:19:08.899
we're working on systems

01:19:08.899 --> 01:19:10.329
so that, they have

01:19:10.329 --> 01:19:12.630
as, as you've seen really elegant ways of 

01:19:12.630 --> 01:19:16.180
I-tracking in infants and we can record their brain activity

01:19:16.180 --> 01:19:19.280
and what they're looking at at the same time. And the idea is to kind of really do

01:19:19.280 --> 01:19:20.679
a broad,

01:19:20.679 --> 01:19:22.730
a broad, broad measures

01:19:22.730 --> 01:19:23.910
across 

01:19:23.910 --> 01:19:25.380
domains of perceptions,

01:19:25.380 --> 01:19:29.999
so looking at visual information by comparing faces and toys, looking at

01:19:29.999 --> 01:19:31.770
auditory information by comparing

01:19:31.770 --> 01:19:33.839
voices, and then

01:19:33.839 --> 01:19:35.010
as me and Warren

01:19:35.010 --> 01:19:41.050
are, are inclined to do, using more nationalistic
stimuli, so of examples of mothers and other people talking

01:19:41.050 --> 01:19:43.040
and objects moving to music 

01:19:43.040 --> 01:19:45.259
and examining in real time the brain response 

01:19:45.259 --> 01:19:47.769
to that. So I want quickly acknowledge

01:19:47.769 --> 01:19:50.840
all the people who have helped with this research, most

01:19:50.840 --> 01:19:54.540
importantly the children with autism and their families who come in

01:19:54.540 --> 01:19:55.550
and 

01:19:55.550 --> 01:19:57.550
tolerate all the things that we ask them to do

01:19:57.550 --> 01:19:58.829
in the hope that we're going to

01:19:58.829 --> 01:20:03.309
help future generations of children with autism. Many colleagues here at Yale,

01:20:03.309 --> 01:20:08.410
colleagues in other places, and to Nora and Agnus, who, although they didn't have a say 

01:20:08.410 --> 01:20:10.680
in the matter, will have retrospectively

01:20:10.680 --> 01:20:12.300
permitted me to, to plaster their face all over the internet. Thank you.

